==> ap242-gauge/README.md <==
# ap242-gauge

ap242-gauge opens a STEP AP242 file (for example from CATIA V5) in your browser.
It measures distances, angles, diameters and radii of features from a reference feature.

It is one HTML file that works offline.
It needs no server and no network.
It uses occt-import-js (WebAssembly) to read the STEP file.

## Use

1. Download [`dist/ap242-gauge.html`](https://github.com/yujieteo/ap242-gauge/raw/main/dist/ap242-gauge.html) (about 10 MB).
   Open it in a current browser (Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari).
   It works from a local file.
2. Click **Open STEP file**, or drop a `.stp` or `.step` file on the page.
   All parts of the file are shown.
3. Select a pick mode and click a reference feature (A), then a second feature (B):
   - **Face**: a plane, or a cylinder (axis, diameter and radius).
     Other faces (cone, torus, free form) give their centroid.
   - **Edge**: the boundary of the clicked face nearest to the click, as a line or a circle (centre, axis, diameter).
   - **Point**: the mesh vertex nearest to the click.
4. The **Measure** table shows, as applicable:
   - plane to plane: angle, and distance when the planes are parallel
   - plane to axis: angle, and distance when the axis is parallel to the plane
   - axis to axis: angle, and distance between parallel axes or shortest distance between skew axes
   - point to plane, point to axis, and the plain point-to-point distance between the two pick points (with dx, dy, dz)

Lengths are in the length unit of the STEP file (mm, cm, m, inch or foot), read from its `LENGTH_UNIT`.
Angles are in degrees.
Drag to rotate, right-drag or shift-drag to pan, use the wheel to zoom.

## How it measures

occt-import-js tessellates each B-rep face of the STEP file into triangles.
The mesh vertices lie on the exact CAD surface.
The tool fits the vertices of one face:

- plane: least squares (smallest eigenvector of the point covariance)
- cylinder: the axis is the direction normal to all triangle normals, and the radius comes from a least squares circle in the plane normal to the axis
- edge: the boundary segments of the face, grown along the boundary, fitted to a line or a circle

<img src="docs/figures/face-fit.svg" width="640" alt="Three panels. a: a plane patch of mesh triangles with its normal n, the direction of least spread of the vertices. b: a faceted cylinder whose triangle normals all point across the axis. c: the view along the axis, where the vertices lie on a least squares circle of radius r.">

*How one face is fitted: (a) plane normal n, (b) cylinder axis normal to all triangle normals, (c) radius r from a circle across the axis.
The 12 facets are illustrative.*

Each fit shows its rms residual.
A face fits a plane or a cylinder only when the residual is below 1e-4 of the face size.

## Build and test

You need [Bun](https://bun.sh) 1.4.4.

```sh
bun install
bun run build   # writes dist/ap242-gauge.html
bun test        # no browser
bun run check   # type check, source rules, dead code, tests, dist, page budget, output replay
```

`build.ts` puts into one HTML file: `occt-import-js.js`, the `occt-import-js.wasm` file (base64) and `src/app.ts` with its imports, bundled by `bun build`.
The build is deterministic, because it does not minify or compress.
CI rebuilds and checks that the committed `dist/` is the same.

The test (`test/nist.test.ts`) loads two NIST AP242 test models with occt-import-js, fits the faces that the NIST drawings dimension and compares the values with the drawing values: within 0.001 file units for lengths and 0.01° for angles.
See [`test/data/SOURCE.md`](test/data/SOURCE.md) for the files and their source.

`bun run replay` runs the tests on the working tree and records the input of each fit and measurement.
It runs these inputs through `src/geom` on the base commit and on the working tree, and fails on each difference.
Run `bun run replay -- --accept` to accept a change of the output after you read the diff.

`bun run scripts/figure.ts` renders `docs/figures/face-fit.ts` with the schematic-figure engine and compares the result with `docs/figures/face-fit.svg`.

## Licences

`dist/ap242-gauge.html` contains [occt-import-js](https://github.com/kovacsv/occt-import-js) 0.0.23 by Viktor Kovacs, under the **GNU Lesser General Public License v2.1** (LGPL-2.1). occt-import-js contains Open CASCADE Technology, under LGPL-2.1 with the Open CASCADE exception.
The HTML file holds both licence texts (button **Licences**) and a notice at the top.
To use a different build of occt-import-js, replace it in `node_modules/occt-import-js` and run `bun run build`.

The NIST test files are a work of the US Government.
==> femahoy/AGENTS.md <==
# femahoy: Rules for Build Agents

This file is for the agents that build femahoy itself.
The product design is in `DESIGN.md`.

---

## 1. Dependencies

Do not add a dependency unless the owner approves it.
Every dependency must be under MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD, ISC, Zlib, or a similar permissive licence.
Never link GPL or AGPL code.

## 2. Forbidden

- Copy text, `.inp` files, figures, or numbers from the Abaqus manuals.
- Add any Dassault Systèmes file to the repo.
- Use "Abaqus" as a product name or in a product name.
  Abaqus is a trademark of Dassault Systèmes.
- Put secrets, API keys, or tokens in code, fixtures, logs, or commits.
- Weaken, skip, or delete a validation case or a test so that a change can pass.
- Change the tolerance of an existing case without a written reason in its fixture file and the approval of the owner.
  A new case with a written tolerance budget in its fixture file needs no approval.
  Reason: a wider tolerance can hide a fault that an existing case found.
  A new case has no earlier result to hide.

## 3. Bugs

A bug fix adds a test only when the bug can come back.
Write that test first.
Show the failing output.
Fix the bug.
Show the passing output.
==> femahoy/README.md <==
# femahoy

femahoy is a chat for finite element and CFD analyses.
You ask for an analysis, and Claude Code, signed in with your own Claude subscription, does the work on your computer.

<p align="center"><img src="docs/figures/architecture.svg" width="320" alt="Diagram of the parts of femahoy. The browser, with the chat and the 3D viewer, sends HTTP requests with the token to fh serve at 127.0.0.1:8790. On your computer, fh serve starts Claude Code with your own login. Claude Code runs the fh commands to mesh, solve and report. The fh commands write the runs and results to the state folder, and fh serve reads them."></p>

fh serve starts Claude Code, Claude Code runs the fh commands, and fh serve shows what they write to the state folder.

## Start

Do these 4 steps on macOS (Apple Silicon) or Linux (x86-64 or ARM64).

1. Install femahoy and the solvers for your user.
   You need no root and no Nix.

   ```sh
   git clone https://github.com/yujieteo/femahoy
   cd femahoy
   scripts/install.sh
   ```

   The script copies `fh` to `~/.local/bin`.
   If this folder is not on PATH, the script prints the line to add to your shell start file.
   See "Install" below.

2. Install Claude Code and sign in with your Claude subscription.
   If `claude auth status` already shows a login, skip this step.

   ```sh
   curl -fsSL https://claude.ai/install.sh | bash
   claude
   ```

   femahoy reads no credential.
   The login stays in Claude Code.

3. Start the page.

   ```sh
   fh serve
   ```

   On macOS, `fh serve` opens the page in your browser.
   On Linux, open the address that it prints.
   At start, `fh serve` checks that Claude Code starts and has a login.
   If not, the chat tells you what to do.

   The address is the same at each start: `http://127.0.0.1:8790/?token=TOKEN`.
   Set `FH_PORT` to use a different port.
   If the port is in use, `fh serve` stops with an error.
   The token is in the file `token` of the state folder.
   Keep the address private, because each person with the address can use the page.
   To make a new token, remove the file `token` and start `fh serve` again.

   To open the page on a phone, forward a local port of the phone to `127.0.0.1:8790` on the computer through SSH, for example with a local port forward in Termius.
   On a computer, use `ssh -L 8790:127.0.0.1:8790 HOST`.
   Then open the same address on the phone.
   The page accepts only the host `127.0.0.1:8790`, so the local port must also be 8790.

4. Ask for an analysis in the chat.
   For example: "Build and solve a steel cantilever of 100 x 10 x 10 mm with 1 MPa on the top face".

   The agent writes a CAD script for the part, meshes it with gmsh, writes the solver input and solves it.
   It solves at least 2 meshes to find the mesh error.
   The chat shows each command of the agent, and the viewer beside the chat shows the model and the results.
   At the end, the agent writes 1 HTML report with the results, a hand check and the viewer.

Stop `fh serve` with Ctrl+C.
The chat ignores your personal Claude Code setup (`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, settings, hooks, plugins, MCP servers and connectors).
Thus each person gets the same agent.

## Analysis types

The agent has instructions for each analysis type in this table.
Validation cases check each type against a published or measured reference.

| Analysis type | Example case | The case checks |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Structures | [contact-hertz](validation/reports/contact-hertz.md) | The largest contact pressure of a steel ball on a flat steel block, against the Hertz theory |
| CFD | [naca23012-flap](validation/reports/naca23012-flap.md) | The lift and the pitching moment of an airfoil with a plain flap at 3 flap angles, against wind tunnel tests |
| Electromagnetics | [team7-eddy-current](validation/reports/team7-eddy-current.md) | The eddy currents at 50 Hz in an aluminium plate with a hole under a coil, against measurements |
| Acoustics | [acoustics-pulsating-sphere](validation/reports/acoustics-pulsating-sphere.md) | The sound that a pulsating sphere radiates into air at 500 Hz, against the analytical solution |
| Fluid-structure interaction | [turek-hron-fsi1](validation/reports/turek-hron-fsi1.md) | The steady shape of a soft flag behind a cylinder in a channel flow, against the Turek-Hron benchmark |
| Topology optimization | [topology-mbb-beam](validation/reports/topology-mbb-beam.md) | The minimum compliance of the half MBB beam with 50 % of the volume, against a published SIMP code |
| Shape and parameter optimization | [shape-hole-biaxial](validation/reports/shape-hole-biaxial.md) | The hole of minimum stress concentration in a plate under biaxial stress, against the exact optimum |
| Random vibration | [random-vibration-cantilever](validation/reports/random-vibration-cantilever.md) | The RMS tip acceleration of a cantilever on a shaker, against the modal sum of the beam theory |
| Multibody dynamics | [slider-crank](validation/reports/slider-crank.md) | The motion of a slider-crank with a motor, against the closed-form solution |
| Particles (SPH, DEM) | [dam-break-martin-moyce](validation/reports/dam-break-martin-moyce.md) | The surge front of a 2D dam break, against the measurements of Martin and Moyce |
| Explicit dynamics (impact) | [taylor-bar-impact](validation/reports/taylor-bar-impact.md) | The final length and the mushroom radius of a copper bar after an impact on a rigid wall, against a published benchmark |
| Optics and photonics | [optics-mie-cylinder](validation/reports/optics-mie-cylinder.md) | The scatter of a plane wave of light on a dielectric cylinder, against the analytical series solution |
| Antennas | [dipole-king-middleton](validation/reports/dipole-king-middleton.md) | The input impedance and the far field of a centre-fed dipole, against the theory of King and Middleton |

Other structures cases check buckling, plasticity, rubber, cracks, composite laminates, shells, natural frequencies, a sudden load, heat transfer and measured warpage.
Other CFD cases check natural convection, conjugate heat transfer, a free surface and a supersonic shock.

`validation/reports/index.md` links the report of each case: the checks, the derivation and the plots of the runs.
Each report is 1 Markdown file, with each plot as an SVG file.
`scripts/validation-reports.py` makes the reports again.
In the root of the clone, `fh validate` runs all the cases.

The table in section 6 of `DESIGN.md` gives the solver and the input of each analysis type.

## Your own model

Give `fh run` a CalculiX `.inp` file or the case folder of another analysis type.
`fh run` starts the solver itself and needs no daemon.
The page of `fh serve` shows the model and the results of each run.
Its page `/reports` lists the reports of the runs and opens each one.
In a terminal, for example over SSH, `fh runs` prints the solver, the status, the time and the key result of each run.
`fh mesh` makes the mesh and the sets from a STEP file.
`fh report` writes 1 HTML file with a text, the viewer, the results of runs and plots of curves (CSV files of x-y values).
The file opens with a double-click.
`fh fit` fits a model to a curve, for example the constants of a rubber for CalculiX.
`fh --help` lists the commands.

## Install

`scripts/install.sh` needs bash, git, tar, and curl or wget.
On macOS, it also needs the Xcode command line tools (`xcode-select --install`).
It installs no system package, and it does not install Claude Code.

- The script builds `fh` and the browser viewer from this clone.
  It installs gmsh and the solver of each analysis type from conda-forge or from the release files of the solver.
  It builds some solvers from source.
  The head of the script names each source.
  The script checks each download with its SHA-256.
- All of it goes to `~/.local/share/femahoy` (`$XDG_DATA_HOME/femahoy`), except the OpenFOAM adapter of preCICE on macOS, which goes to the user folder of OpenFOAM (`~/OpenFOAM`).
  In the test runs, the folder used 11 to 14 GB.
  The log of each solver is in the folder `logs` there.
- `~/.local/bin`, or the folder of `--prefix DIR`, gets `fh`, the viewer, `fh-python` (the Python of the CAD scripts, with build123d) and `solvers.env`.
  `solvers.env` has 1 line `FH_NAME=PATH` for each solver that the script installed.
  `fh` reads this file at start.
- To update, run `git pull` and `scripts/install.sh` again.
  A second run builds `fh` again and keeps each solver that has the same version.
- Each [GitHub release](https://github.com/yujieteo/femahoy/releases) has `fh` and the viewer for Linux, with no solver.
  `CHANGELOG.md` gives the changes of each release.
  To install a release with the solvers, run `git checkout vX.Y.Z` and then `scripts/install.sh`.
  `fh --version` prints the version.
- At the end, the script names each solver that it did not install, with the analysis types that it disables.
  On Linux ARM64, OpenFOAM and GetDP have no build, so CFD, fluid-structure interaction, electromagnetics and acoustics are not available there.

To use your own solver, for example from Nix, set its variable (see "Environment variables") before you run the script.
The script then does not install that solver, and `fh` uses the variable, because a variable of the environment has priority over `solvers.env`.
`scripts/ccx-precice.nix` builds `ccx_preCICE` with Nix. preCICE also needs its OpenFOAM adapter, which the script builds only for the OpenFOAM that it installs.

## Environment variables

A solver variable has priority over its line in `solvers.env`.
With neither, `fh` uses the program of the list below on PATH, or `python3` for a Python solver.

- `FH_GMSH`: the gmsh program of `fh mesh`, and of `fh run` and `fh validate` for a GetDP case.
- `FH_CCX`: the `ccx` program of `fh run` and `fh validate`.
- `FH_OPENFOAM`: the `openfoam` program of `fh run` and `fh validate`.
  It runs each program of OpenFOAM as `$FH_OPENFOAM PROGRAM ARGS`.
- `FH_GETDP`: the `getdp` program of `fh run` and `fh validate`.
- `FH_PYCHRONO`: the Python with PyChrono of `fh run` and `fh validate`.
- `FH_MEEP`: the Python with Meep of `fh run` and `fh validate`.
- `FH_CCX_PRECICE`: the `ccx_preCICE` program (CalculiX with the preCICE adapter) of `fh run` and `fh validate`.
- `FH_LAMMPS`: the `lmp` program of `fh run` and `fh validate`.
- `FH_OPENEMS`: the Python with openEMS of `fh run` and `fh validate`.
- `FH_SKEIN`: the `skein` program of `fh run`.
  The default is `skein` on `PATH`.
- `FH_STATE`: the state folder of `fh run`, `fh report` and `fh serve`.
- `FH_VIEWER`: the folder of the wasm viewer of `fh report` and `fh serve`.
- `FH_PORT`: the port of `fh serve` on 127.0.0.1.
  The default is 8790.
- `FH_NO_OPEN`: with a value that is not empty and not `0`, `fh serve` does not open the browser.

## Documents

- `DESIGN.md`: the current rules of the product, each with its reason.
- `AGENTS.md`: rules for the agents that build femahoy.
- [Input keywords and element types](docs/reference/inp.md): the input of the preview and the element types of the page.
- [Check file fields](docs/reference/check-toml.md): measurements and tolerances of a validation case.
- [Validation cases as 1 HTML file](docs/reference/quine.md): 5 cases that run offline in a browser, with their check.

femahoy is under the MIT License, see `LICENSE`.
