==> goosestrike/README.md <==
# goosestrike

A bird strike simulation on a stiffened fuselage panel: an explicit dynamics finite element solver that runs in the browser.
Live page: https://teoyujie.org/play/goosestrike/

A 4 lb bird hits a stiffened aluminium fuselage panel at V<sub>C</sub>, the load case of 14 CFR 25.571(e)(1).
The solver is explicit dynamics, written in [Goose](https://github.com/aardappel/goose), compiled through C to WebAssembly.
If the browser has WebGPU, the same solver runs on the GPU: WGSL compute shaders do each time step, and Goose makes the set-up and controls the GPU.
The GPU solver runs finer meshes in seconds, and the page lets you change the bird speed and the skin thickness.
It runs in a browser page that shows the bird as a cartoon goose, shows the Goose code that runs, and exports the strike as an animated GIF.

![The goose hits the panel](docs/strike.png)

Run it:

```sh
python3 -m http.server -d web 8000
# then open http://localhost:8000/
```

A local server has no `/shared/kowalski.css`, so the page shows without the fonts, colours, button look and focus ring of the site.
The buttons show as plain browser buttons.
The repository has the built `web/strike.wasm` and `web/strike-gpu.wasm`, so the page needs no build step.
The page uses the GPU solver if the browser has WebGPU and the GPU solver starts, else the CPU solver.
Add `?solver=cpu` to the URL to use the CPU solver.
This is not a certification analysis.
The page and this README say where the model is coarse.

## The load case

14 CFR 25.571(e)(1), as amended to Amendment 25-148 ([text at law.cornell.edu](https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/14/25.571)):

> (e) Damage-tolerance (discrete source) evaluation.
> The airplane must be capable of successfully completing a flight during which likely structural damage occurs as a result of— (1) Impact with a 4-pound bird when the velocity of the airplane relative to the bird along the airplane's flight path is equal to V<sub>C</sub> at sea level or 0.85V<sub>C</sub> at 8,000 feet, whichever is more critical;

| Quantity | Value | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Bird mass | 4 lb = 1.81436948 kg | the rule, and 1 lb = 0.45359237 kg |
| V<sub>C</sub> | 350 KCAS | 14 CFR 25.1505: V<sub>MO</sub> must not be greater than V<sub>C</sub>. The A320 has V<sub>MO</sub> = 350 kt ([EASA TCDS EASA.A.064](https://www.easa.europa.eu/en/document-library/type-certificates/aircraft-cs-25-cs-22-cs-23-cs-vla-cs-lsa/easaa064-airbus-a318-a319-a320), Issue 62, Section 1 A320). Thus 350 KCAS is the lowest V<sub>C</sub> that the rule allows for this aeroplane. |
| Impact speed | 180.06 m/s | 350 KCAS = 350 KTAS at sea level ISA. 1 kt = 1852/3600 m/s. |
| Altitude | sea level | 0.85 V<sub>C</sub> at 8000 ft is about 0.96 V<sub>C</sub> true airspeed, so the sea-level case is more critical. |
| Direction | normal to the skin, at the middle of the panel | an idealized worst case |

## The bird: physics and costume

The physics bird is the standard substitute bird of bird strike analysis.
The cartoon goose is only paint on it.

| Physics | Value | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Shape | cylinder with hemispherical ends, length/diameter = 2 | Heimbs 2011, Perdikoulis et al. 2021 |
| Size | diameter 113.1 mm, length 226.2 mm | from the mass and the density |
| Material | gelatin with 10 % porosity, 950 kg/m³ | Perdikoulis et al. 2021, after Barber et al. |
| Equation of state | Tait for water: p = (ρ<sub>0</sub>c<sub>0</sub>²/γ)((ρ/ρ<sub>0</sub>)<sup>γ</sup> − 1), c<sub>0</sub> = 1480 m/s, γ = 7, no tension (p ≥ 0) | Heimbs 2011 |

The costume (white feathers, eyes, beak, wings, feet, head tuft and blush) follows the particles, but it has no mass and no effect on the result.
The flight before contact is a cartoon.
The simulation starts with the nose 2 mm plus half a particle spacing from the skin.

## The panel

The geometry is a representative assumption, not a drawing of a real aeroplane.

| Part | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Fuselage radius | 1.975 m (A320 class) |
| Panel | 1.0668 m along the fuselage (two 533 mm frame bays) × 0.760 m around it (four 190 mm stringer bays) |
| Edges | clamped, on the next frames and stringers |
| Skin | 2024-T3, 1.6 mm (the page can set 0.8 mm to 3.2 mm) |
| Stringers | 4 Z sections, 7075-T6, web 25.4 mm, flange 15 mm, 1.6 mm |
| Frames | 2 Z sections, 7075-T6, web 76.2 mm, flange 25 mm, 1.6 mm |

Material data, from ASM Aerospace Specification Metals (data sheets for 2024-T3 and 7075-T6 sheet, as published on MatWeb):

| | ρ (kg/m³) | E (GPa) | ν | yield (MPa) | ultimate (MPa) | elongation |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 2024-T3 | 2780 | 73.1 | 0.33 | 345 | 483 | 18 % |
| 7075-T6 | 2810 | 71.7 | 0.33 | 503 | 572 | 11 % |

The plasticity is bilinear: a straight line from yield to the ultimate stress at the elongation.
An element erodes when its plastic strain reaches the elongation less the elastic strain at the ultimate stress: 17.3 % for 2024-T3 and 10.2 % for 7075-T6.

## The method

All of it is in `sim/strike.goose`.
Units are SI.

- Bird: smoothed particle hydrodynamics (SPH).
  Cubic spline kernel, h = 1.2 × particle spacing, continuity equation for the density, Monaghan artificial viscosity (α = 1, β = 2).
- Panel: Belytschko-Lin-Tsay 4-node shells with 5 Gauss points through the thickness.
  J2 plasticity with linear hardening and the plane-stress return of Simo and Taylor.
  Stiffness hourglass control.
  Lumped masses.
- Contact: penalty.
  Each particle pushes on its nearest shell element, with a stiffness of 0.1 × reduced mass / dt².
- Time: central differences.
  The stable time step comes from the smallest shell element and from the SPH sound speed (CFL 0.25 for SPH, 0.9 for the shells).
- Memory: all state is fixed-size global arrays in static data.
  The time loop allocates nothing.

The energy balance closes: bird kinetic energy + bird internal energy + panel kinetic energy + panel internal work stays within 0.4 % of the initial kinetic energy in all runs below.

## Accuracy: the rigid wall check

Wilbeck (1978) gives two pressures at the centre of a soft body that hits a rigid wall:

- the Hugoniot shock pressure at first contact, P<sub>H</sub> = ρ<sub>0</sub> U<sub>s</sub> v.
  Here U<sub>s</sub> comes from the jump conditions with the same Tait equation of state.
- the stagnation pressure of the steady flow after the shock, P<sub>s</sub> = ½ ρ<sub>0</sub> v².

At 180.06 m/s, P<sub>H</sub> = 314.4 MPa (U<sub>s</sub> = 1838 m/s) and P<sub>s</sub> = 15.40 MPa.
The solver measures the force on a disc of radius D/8 (14.2 mm) at the impact centre.
"Peak" is the maximum.
"Plateau" is the mean from 0.25 to 0.75 of the squash-up time L/v = 1260 µs.

| Bird spacing | Particles | Peak (MPa) | Peak vs P<sub>H</sub> | Plateau (MPa) | Plateau vs P<sub>s</sub> | Impulse / m v | Energy balance |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 9 mm | 2560 | 180.5 | −42.6 % | 19.37 | +25.8 % | 1.0079 | 0.9997 |
| 6 mm | 8760 | 317.4 | +0.9 % | 19.67 | +27.7 % | 0.9955 | 1.0006 |
| 4.5 mm | 20902 | 322.9 | +2.7 % | 17.42 | +13.1 % | 0.9935 | 1.0000 |
| 3.5 mm | 44412 | 350.1 | +11.3 % | 15.90 | +3.2 % | 0.9921 | 1.0000 |

The impulse is m v within 1 %, and the energy balance closes within 0.1 %.
The plateau converges to P<sub>s</sub> as the spacing decreases, to +3.2 % at 3.5 mm.
The peak converges less well.
At 9 mm, the smoothing length (10.9 mm) smears the shock, and the peak is 43 % low.
At 6 mm and 4.5 mm the peak is within 3 %, and at 3.5 mm it is 11 % high.
The peak is the maximum of one time step on a 14 mm disc, so it is noisy.
Use it as an order-of-magnitude check, and the plateau as the convergence check.
These numbers come from `validate`, the native build of the same solver (see below).
The page runs the same check in the browser with the "Run the wall check" button.

## The panel result

Impact at 180.06 m/s, run to 3 ms. Bird kinetic energy at the start: 29.41 kJ.

| Bird spacing | Skin cells along the fuselage | Shells | Peak contact force | Max deflection | Eroded skin shells | Eroded stiffener shells | Panel work | Hourglass part | Worst energy balance error |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 9 mm | 56 | 3088 | 240.1 kN | 150.8 mm | 0 | 36 | 18.53 kJ | 0.34 kJ | 0.32 % |
| 6 mm | 88 | 7712 | 229.3 kN | 152.1 mm | 8 | 62 | 18.92 kJ | 0.25 kJ | 0.20 % |
| 4.5 mm | 112 | 12960 | 209.1 kN | 152.9 mm | 25 | 152 | 19.29 kJ | 0.27 kJ | 0.11 % |
| 3.5 mm | 144 | 20560 | 203.7 kN | 228.1 mm | 249 | 191 | 18.47 kJ | 0.38 kJ | 0.08 % |

The shell size is about 2.1 bird spacings.
The panel mass is 5.05 kg.

From 9 mm to 4.5 mm, the deflection changes by less than 2 %.
The skin damage does not converge: at 9 mm, no skin element reaches the failure strain, but at 6 mm and 4.5 mm some do.
At 3.5 mm, 249 skin elements erode, a hole opens in the skin, and the deflection is 50 % larger.
Element erosion depends on the element size, so a tear prediction needs a failure model calibrated to the mesh.
This model has none.
Thus the 3.5 mm result does not show that the real skin tears.
It shows that the erosion model is the coarse part of this model.
The stringers and frames fail locally at all four meshes.
The 3.5 mm row comes from the native build.
The GPU solver gives 227.9 mm to 228.3 mm, 248 to 260 eroded skin shells and 185 to 189 eroded stiffener shells in 5 runs.
The rule accepts damage.
It asks that the aeroplane completes the flight with it, and that needs a residual strength analysis, which this project does not do.

## The browser trade

The GPU solver is 18 to 128 times faster than the CPU solver, and the speed-up increases with the mesh size.
The page offers three meshes on the CPU and four meshes on the GPU.
The solver time per step grows with the particle count, and the stable time step decreases with the element size.

| Page preset | Bird spacing | Skin cells | Bird particles | Shell elements | Steps to 3 ms |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Potato goose | 9 mm | 56 | 2560 | 3088 | 1774 |
| Good goose | 6 mm | 88 | 8760 | 7712 | 2679 |
| Very good goose | 4.5 mm | 112 | 20902 | 12960 | 3594 |
| Excellent goose (GPU only) | 3.5 mm | 144 | 44412 | 20560 | 4652 |

The table below gives the solver time per step in Node 24 on an Apple M5.
The CPU column is the wasm on one core.
The GPU column is the same solver in WebGPU (Dawn on Metal, the `webgpu` package).
The benchmark ran 300 steps on each mesh, the CPU first and then the GPU.
The machine had a load average of 25 from other work, thus the CPU times are higher than on an idle machine.

| Bird spacing | CPU time per step | GPU time per step | Speed-up |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 9 mm | 18.2 ms | 0.99 ms | 18 |
| 6 mm | 76.7 ms | 1.51 ms | 51 |
| 4.5 mm | 146 ms | 1.64 ms | 89 |
| 3.5 mm | 288 ms | 2.25 ms | 128 |

On an idle machine, the CPU times were 5.7 ms, 13.1 ms and 31.3 ms per step for the first three meshes.
The native build is about 1.6 times faster than the wasm.

The table below gives the time of the full panel run on the page, in Chrome 156 on the same machine, with a high load from other work.
The two GPU columns are two different runs.
On the CPU, the page also does the steps one phase at a time for the code view.

| Page preset | CPU | GPU, run 1 | GPU, run 2 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Potato goose | 65 s | 0.9 s | 2.0 s |
| Good goose | 301 s | 3.6 s | 5.6 s |
| Very good goose | 1085 s | 9.7 s | 12.3 s |
| Excellent goose | not available | 20.9 s | 56.7 s |

On the GPU, the 2D canvas renderer limits the page at fine meshes.
At 3.5 mm, the page shows 7 to 15 frames per second, and the solver waits for the frames.

Only Chrome 156 on macOS (Metal) was tested, in headless mode.
Other browsers with WebGPU possibly work, but they were not tested.
Without WebGPU, the page uses the CPU solver.

The 9 mm mesh is the fast default on the CPU.
The trade: its peak pressure is 43 % low, its plateau is 26 % high, and it shows no skin erosion.
But its deflection is within 1.5 % of the 4.5 mm result, and its peak force is 15 % higher.

## The page

- The code view shows `web/strike.goose`, the copy of `sim/strike.goose` that `build.sh` writes.
  The page runs each time step in five phases, one call of the wasm export `step_phase` each: time step, SPH bird, shells (with plasticity and erosion), contact and integration.
  `run_phase` in the source has the five phases.
  The lit lines are the phase that ran last, as `step_phase` returns it.
  The source scrolls to that phase, at most once a second.
  A test checks that the phases one at a time give the same result as whole steps.
  On the GPU, the kernels of `web/strike.wgsl` do the five phases, and the code view says so.
  No line is lit.
- The skin thickness control sets the skin of the next run, from 0.8 mm to 3.2 mm.
  The stringers and frames keep their 1.6 mm.
  The preset values on the page are for the 1.6 mm skin only.
- The telemetry shows the solver (GPU or CPU), the solver time per step and the frame rate.
- "Export GIF" saves the last run as an animated GIF, 480 px wide: about ten frames a second of the approach, then one frame each 25 µs of simulated time.
  Each frame has a caption with the time and the colour bar.
  The encoder is `web/gif.mjs` (median cut palette, LZW), with no dependency.
- The colour maps are continuous ramps, interpolated in OKLab: plastic strain on a square-root scale from the bare metal (0 %) to 18 %, so that the first yield shows, and bird pressure on a log scale from 0.1 MPa to 400 MPa.
- Each shell element has a thin outline, and the panel edges, the free flanges and the lines where a stringer or a frame meets the skin have a heavier outline.
  Thus the shape of the panel shows over the strain colours.
- The look (fonts, palette, interaction rules) is that of [teoyujie.org](https://teoyujie.org): the page links the site's shared stylesheet `/shared/kowalski.css`.
  `web/style.css` has only the rules of this tool.

## Goose on WebAssembly

Goose has no wasm port yet (its `docs/design/wasm.md` is a proposal).
The C that Goose writes compiles with Emscripten, with one risk: Goose reserves a large address range (2 GiB by default) for each data stack, with a guard gap behind it that stops a stack overflow.
WebAssembly linear memory has no reservation and no guard pages.
Emscripten's `mmap` allocates the full size, and `mprotect` does nothing.

The handling:

1. The solver keeps all state in static global arrays.
   The generated C of the time loop (`step_once`, `step_phase` and the functions they call) has no data-stack use.
   You can check this: in the code of the program, `gen/strike.c` uses `->top` only in `gs_init_globals`.
2. `gs_init_globals` builds each zeroed global array in a data-stack temporary and copies it.
   The largest temporary is `esig`, 24000 × 15 × 8 B = 2.88 MB, and each temporary is released before the next one.
3. Thus `build.sh` sets `GS_STACK_RESERVE` to 4 MiB and the guard gap to 64 KiB.
   If a later change makes a larger global array, raise the reserve.

The same is true for `gen/gpu.c`: it uses `->top` only in `gs_init_globals`, and its largest global array is also `esig`.

## Goose on WebGPU

The GPU solver has three parts:

| File | Language | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `sim/gpu.goose` | Goose | The host. It calls `setup` of `strike.goose`, copies the bird and the panel to GPU buffers, makes a bind group for each kernel, records the time steps and reads back the results. It imports `strike.goose`, so the getters of the CPU solver give the GPU results. |
| `sim/gpu.c` | C | The WebGPU calls, through `webgpu.h`. Goose cannot make the `webgpu.h` descriptors (they hold pointers) or give a callback, so `sim/gpu.goose` calls these C functions as `extern fn`. This file has no solver logic. |
| `web/strike.wgsl` | WGSL | 11 compute kernels. One time step is 10 kernels in sequence: the five phases of `run_phase` in `strike.goose`, and a reduction that sums the energies and the forces, advances the time and computes the next time step. |

The WebGPU implementation is Emscripten's own port, `--use-port=emdawnwebgpu` (from the Dawn releases).
The page gives the GPU device to the wasm module, so no C code waits for a promise.

The GPU solver is a port of the CPU solver, with these differences:

- It uses f32, because WGSL has no f64.
  The CPU solver uses f64.
  The plasticity return stops at a tolerance of 1e-6, not 1e-12.
- The SPH forces are a gather: each particle sums the forces from its neighbours, so each pair is computed two times.
  The CPU solver computes each pair one time.
- The shell and contact forces on the nodes are sums with atomic operations, so the sequence of the additions changes from run to run.
  Thus the GPU results are not bit-for-bit repeatable.
- The page keeps two batches of steps on the GPU.
  Thus the GPU does the next batch while the page reads the results of the last one.

`test/gpu.test.mjs` runs the 9 mm panel to 3 ms on the GPU and on the CPU, and compares them.
The tolerances are about 10 times the differences on Metal:

| Value | Tolerance | Difference on Metal |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Max deflection, at each step | 0.1 mm | 0.013 mm |
| Peak contact force | 1 % | less than 0.001 % |
| Max deflection, max plastic strain, panel work | 0.1 mm, 1 %, 1 % | 0.0001 mm, 0.03 %, 0.003 % |
| Eroded shells (all, and skin) | 2 elements | 0 |
| Steps to 3 ms | 1 % | 0 |

The test also compares what the page draws: the plastic strain of each element within 0.01, and the node positions within 5 mm.
Both checks skip the eroded elements and their nodes, because these nodes move differently from run to run on the GPU.
The test also runs the rigid wall on the GPU: the impulse is m v within 2 %, and the energy balance closes within 1 %.

The GPU and the CPU agree less well on the plateau of the rigid wall check.
The centre sensor force changes by about 20 % from step to step, so small differences change its mean.

| Bird spacing | Peak vs P<sub>H</sub>, CPU / GPU | Plateau vs P<sub>s</sub>, CPU / GPU | Impulse / m v, CPU / GPU |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 9 mm | −42.6 % / −42.6 % | +25.8 % / +25.8 % | 1.0079 / 1.0079 |
| 6 mm | +0.9 % / +0.9 % | +27.7 % / +25.8 % to +26.5 % | 0.9955 / 0.9957 |
| 4.5 mm | +2.7 % / +2.7 % | +13.1 % / −0.7 % | 0.9935 / 0.9936 |
| 3.5 mm | +11.3 % / +11.3 % | +3.2 % / −0.2 % to 0.0 % | 0.9921 / 0.9920 |

The peak and the impulse agree.
The plateau agrees at 9 mm and 6 mm, but at 4.5 mm and 3.5 mm the GPU plateau is lower.
The cause is not known.
Thus the page shows the plateau of the native CPU solver for each preset.

## Build

The build needs the Goose compiler and Emscripten.
On NixOS, these commands work (no compiler needs to be on the PATH):

```sh
# 1. the Goose compiler, at the commit that this repository uses
git clone https://github.com/aardappel/goose ../goose
git -C ../goose checkout a108677d300cb0e8bbb842c35a353abe67a20c6c
( cd ../goose && nix shell nixpkgs#cmake nixpkgs#clang nixpkgs#lld nixpkgs#gnumake --command \
    bash -c 'cmake -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++ && cmake --build build -j16' )

# 2. the C (gen/), the wasm (web/strike.mjs, web/strike.wasm, web/strike-gpu.mjs, web/strike-gpu.wasm)
#    and web/strike.goose, with Emscripten 6.0.9. Emscripten downloads its emdawnwebgpu port.
nix shell nixpkgs#emscripten --command env GOOSE=$PWD/../goose/build/goose ./build.sh

# 3. the native validation program
( cd sim && nix shell nixpkgs#clang --command ../../goose/build/goose build -o ../build/validate validate.goose )
build/validate 9000 6000 4500 3500       # rigid wall, bird spacings in µm
build/validate --panel 9000 6000 4500    # the panel
```

Add `--csv` to `validate` to print the time history.

`gen/strike.c` and `gen/strike.h` are the C that the Goose compiler generates from `sim/strike.goose`.
They are C, not C++, and nobody edits them by hand.
They are in the repository so that you can read the code that Emscripten compiles to `web/strike.wasm`.
`gen/gpu.c` and `gen/gpu.h` are the same for `sim/gpu.goose`.
`build.sh` writes them, and writes `gen/sha256sums` with the SHA-256 of the Goose sources, `sim/gpu.c`, the C and the two wasm files.

## Tests

```sh
nix shell nixpkgs#nodejs --command npm ci
nix shell nixpkgs#nodejs --command npm test
```

The tests (`test/strike.test.mjs`) load the committed `web/strike.wasm` in Node and check:

- that the C in `gen/` and the two wasm files come from the current sources (the sums in `gen/sha256sums`), and that `web/strike.goose` is the same as `sim/strike.goose`,
- the load case constants and the Wilbeck reference pressures,
- the rigid wall impulse (m v within 2 %) and energy balance (within 1 %),
- the panel energy balance (within 1 %), the panel mass, and that a new set-up gives the same result,
- that the phases one at a time (`step_phase`) give the same result as whole steps (`advance`).

`test/gpu.test.mjs` compares the GPU solver with the CPU solver (see "Goose on WebGPU").
It gets WebGPU in Node from the `webgpu` package (Dawn), a development dependency.
If there is no GPU adapter, these tests skip, but with `GOOSESTRIKE_GPU=1` they fail.

`test/gif.test.mjs` checks that the GIF palette keeps a colour ramp next to a large light background.

CI runs the same tests on each push.
CI installs lavapipe, a Vulkan driver that runs on the CPU, and sets `GOOSESTRIKE_GPU=1`.
Thus CI also runs the GPU tests.

## Sources

- 14 CFR 25.571(e)(1) and 25.1505.
  Electronic Code of Federal Regulations, through Amendment 25-148.
- EASA Type-Certificate Data Sheet EASA.A.064 (Airbus A318, A319, A320, A321), Issue 62.
- R.
  L.
  Wilbeck, *Impact behavior of low strength projectiles*, AFML-TR-77-134, 1978 (DTIC ADA060423).
- S.
  Heimbs, Computational methods for bird strike simulations: a review, *Computers & Structures* 89 (2011) 2093–2112, doi:10.1016/j.compstruc.2011.08.007.
- C.
  Perdikoulis, E.
  Theotokoglou, G.
  Giannopoulos, Bird strike virtual testing simulations and results, for preliminary airframe design structural optimization, WCCM-ECCOMAS 2020 (2021).
- ASM Aerospace Specification Metals, data sheets for aluminium 2024-T3 and 7075-T6.

## Licence

MIT (see `LICENSE`), for the code of this repository.
Goose is Apache-2.0.
This repository does not include Goose source, but the generated `gen/strike.c` and `web/strike.wasm` contain the Goose runtime that the compiler puts in its output.
Thus `web/GOOSE-LICENSE.txt` has the Goose licence.
`web/strike-gpu.mjs` and `web/strike-gpu.wasm` contain code of Emscripten's emdawnwebgpu port.
`web/WEBGPU-LICENSE.txt` has its BSD licences.
==> marklove-web/README.md <==
# marklove-web

Marklove for the web.
You write one Markdown file, and Marklove makes slides, an article, an EPUB book, subtitles, narration text and packages from it.

This is a port of the Marklove app for iPhone, iPad and Mac.
The core is in [Goose](https://aardappel.github.io/), compiled to C and then to WebAssembly.
The core does the parse, the checks, the render to HTML and MathML, the plots, the themes, find and replace, sam and Vim, the ZIP files and the Git history.
The core also lays out the PDF pages, the slide images and the video frames as SVG, traces the SVG slides, makes the video timeline and writes the MP4 file.
JavaScript does only the page, the browser storage and the browser APIs: canvas, WebCodecs, MediaRecorder and the speech model.

<img src="docs/figures/architecture.svg" width="400" alt="Block diagram. A Markdown file of at most 512 KB goes into the core, which is Goose compiled to C and then to WebAssembly. The core does the parse and checks, HTML and MathML, plots and themes, Find, sam and Vim, the Git history, the page and frame SVG, the ZIP entries and the MP4 header. JavaScript calls the core through the ml_* functions: it sends text and text widths, and gets HTML, SVG and bytes back. JavaScript does the page and editor, IndexedDB, text measurement, canvas, print to PDF, the Kokoro voice, WebCodecs and MediaRecorder. Photos, videos and audio stay in JavaScript as Blobs. The outputs are web pages, EPUB, PDF, PNG and SVG, MP4 and WebM, and the ZIP with .git.">

*The core makes each output.
JavaScript gives it the browser APIs and adds the media bytes, which never go into the core.*

All features are free.
The web version has no subscription, no purchase and no Pro features.

## Use

The `web/` folder is a static site.
It needs no server code and no network after it loads.
The Kokoro voice is the exception: Settings downloads it (about 90 MB), and the browser keeps it.
Narration also needs the network to get kokoro-js from jsDelivr.
Copy the folder to any path on a web server, for example `/play/marklove/`, and open `index.html`.
The app chrome needs the shared stylesheet `/shared/kowalski.css` at the root of the same server.
On teoyujie.org it is there.

To try it on your computer:

```sh
python3 -m http.server --directory web 8000
```

Then open <http://localhost:8000/>.
This server has no `/shared/kowalski.css`.
For local work, serve the `public/` folder of the site repository with this app in it, or accept chrome with no styles.
Projects stay in the storage of your browser (IndexedDB).
To move a project to another browser, export the project ZIP and import it there.

## Build

The repository contains the built files `web/marklove.mjs` and `web/marklove.wasm`.
Build them again after you change a file in `core/`.

You need:

- The Goose compiler.
- Emscripten (`emcc`).

Run:

```sh
GOOSE=/path/to/goose EMCC=/path/to/emcc ./build.sh
```

The script does 2 steps:

1. Goose writes `build/marklove.c` and `build/marklove.h` from `core/marklove.goose`.
2. `emcc` compiles the C to `web/marklove.mjs` and `web/marklove.wasm`.
   It exports each `ml_*` function in the header.

## Test

```sh
node --import ./tests/app_stubs/register.mjs --test tests/*.test.mjs
```

The tests use the built core through `web/core.js`.
The project ZIP test also runs `git fsck` on the exported history when Git is installed.

## Files

| Path | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| `core/marklove.goose` | The functions that JavaScript calls (`ml_*`). |
| `core/parse.goose` | The Markdown parser and the document model. |
| `core/render.goose` | The slides page, the article page and the EPUB book. |
| `core/deck.goose`, `core/sentence.goose` | The Normal and Takahashi slides, sentences and words. |
| `core/narr.goose` | The narration script, the transcript and the subtitles. |
| `core/texmath.goose`, `core/mathtable.goose` | LaTeX to MathML. |
| `core/plot.goose` | Plots as SVG. |
| `core/lint.goose` | The source checks. |
| `core/highlight.goose`, `core/theme.goose` | Source and code colors, and the themes. |
| `core/edit.goose`, `core/vim.goose` | Find and replace, sam commands, the line difference, commit messages, and Vim mode. |
| `core/pack.goose`, `core/gitexport.goose` | ZIP files, the project import, and the Git history. |
| `core/extras.goose` | The equation at the cursor, spoken math, the tour, the prompts and the listener text. |
| `core/layout.goose`, `core/pagesvg.goose` | The page layout of the slides, the video, the article, the handout and the poster, and each page as SVG. |
| `core/trace.goose` | Raster pixels to SVG shapes, for the SVG slide export. |
| `core/video.goose` | The video timeline, the podcast timing and the MP4 file. |
| `core/io.h` | The input buffers, the memory for the Goose data stacks, and the text and math measurement in the browser. |
| `web/core.js` | The JavaScript interface to the core. |
| `web/index.html`, `web/app.js`, `web/app.css` | The app page, its start and its styles. |
| `web/store.js`, `web/ui.js`, `web/editor.js`, `web/find.js`, `web/preview.js`, `web/export.js`, `web/panels.js` | The app parts: storage and settings, controls, the editor, Find and Vim, the preview and presentation, export, and the other panels. |
| `web/pages.js`, `web/video.js`, `web/voice.js` | The PDF pages, the PNG and SVG slides, the video and audio encoders, and the Kokoro voice. |
| `web/demo-peafowl.png` | The photo of the tour project. |
| `web/style.css`, `web/runtime.js`, `web/article.css`, `web/article.js` | The parts of the exported pages, based on Marklove. `web/style.css` and `web/article.css` add the shared interaction rules: press scale, a hover fade only on a fine pointer, and no tap highlight. Thus they differ from the native app. |
| `web/fonts/` | Courier Prime, Courier Prime Sans, Libre Baskerville and KpMath, with their licenses (SIL Open Font License). |

The Kokoro voice uses the web version of [kokoro-js](https://github.com/hexgrad/kokoro/tree/main/kokoro.js) 1.2.1 from jsDelivr.
It runs the Kokoro-82M ONNX model (`onnx-community/Kokoro-82M-v1.0-ONNX`, q8) from Hugging Face.
Both are under the Apache License 2.0.

## Limits

- The Markdown file can have a maximum size of 512 KB.
  Each Goose data stack has a fixed 8 MB in WebAssembly, and WebAssembly has no guard pages.
  Thus the core refuses a larger source, so that a stack cannot overwrite other data.
  The same limit applies to each text that goes into the core, and to each result of Find, Replace, sam, Vim substitution and Vim put.
  Find and sam keep a maximum of 100000 matches.
  Marklove does not keep a text that is larger than 512 KB as a version.
  With Vim mode on and a text larger than 512 KB, Vim goes to Insert mode and each key goes to the browser until the text is smaller.
  A long talk is less than 100 KB.
- Photos, videos and audio stay in JavaScript as Blobs.
  They never go into the core.
  The core writes the ZIP and Git entries for them, and JavaScript adds the bytes.
  The same applies to the encoded video and audio samples: the core writes the MP4 header, and JavaScript adds the samples.
- The SVG export traces regions with millions of pixels.
  The core keeps the arrays for these pixels in the C heap, not in a data stack.
- Encoders: browsers encode AAC only at 44.1 or 48 kHz, so the 24 kHz voice is encoded at 48 kHz.
  Without AAC, the audio is Opus in the MP4 file.
  Without WebCodecs, MediaRecorder records the video or the podcast in real time as WebM.

## Feature parity

| Marklove feature | Web status |
| --- | --- |
| Projects: make, rename, duplicate, delete | Yes. The projects are in the browser storage. |
| Markdown editor with Courier Prime, line count and wrap | Yes. |
| Source colors in the editor | Yes. |
| Save each change at once, recovery of text that is not saved | Yes. Each change goes to the browser storage. |
| Insert menu (Slide, Photo or Video, Equation, Speaker Notes, Pause, Title and Details) | Yes. |
| Equation preview with spoken text | Yes. |
| Find and Replace: Sam, Vim and plain text, the sam command line | Yes. |
| Sam Tutorial | Yes. |
| Vim mode | Yes. |
| Source checks with Go to Source | Yes. |
| Preview in the Normal and the Takahashi style | Yes. The preview is the interactive web page. |
| Speaker Ready (current and next slide, notes, timer) | Yes, in a second window. |
| Listener Preview | Yes. |
| Themes and accent colors | Yes. |
| Versions: automatic commits with commit messages, changes, restore | Yes. The versions are in the browser storage. The project ZIP holds them as a real `.git` history. |
| Import a project ZIP | Yes. The `.git` history in the ZIP is not imported: the project starts with a new history. |
| The tour and the welcome | Yes. |
| Export: interactive web page, article web page, EPUB | Yes. |
| Export: article Markdown, revised Markdown, narration script, transcript, subtitles, prompt text | Yes. |
| Export: presenter ZIP, listener ZIP, project ZIP with `.git` | Yes. |
| Export: slides PDF, article PDF, handout, poster | Yes. The core lays out the pages as the app does, and the browser print dialog saves them as PDF. The browser measures the text with the same fonts. |
| Export: slides as PNG images or SVG files | Yes. A canvas draws the PNG images. For SVG, the core traces the text, photos, equations and plots into vector shapes, as the app does. |
| Export: video, narrated video, math animation video, podcast | Yes. WebCodecs encodes H.264 and AAC, and the core writes the MP4 file. Without WebCodecs, MediaRecorder makes a WebM file. |
| Narration voice (Kokoro on the device) | Yes. The voice is the kokoro-js web version of Kokoro-82M. It runs in the browser. The web version has 28 English voices. For a voice that it does not have, it uses `af_heart`. |
| iCloud sync and merge between devices | Not possible on the web. iCloud Drive is available only to apps on Apple platforms. |
| Command line tool and Shortcuts actions | Not possible on the web. A web page cannot add a command to Terminal or to the Shortcuts app. |
| VoiceOver rotors, Dynamic Type, Bold Text | Not possible on the web. These are Apple system features. The web page uses the zoom, the screen reader and the reduced motion setting of the browser. |
| Languages other than English | Not yet. The app text is in Xcode string catalogs, and the web version has only the English text. |
| Marklove Pro purchase (StoreKit) | Removed. All features are free. |
==> neurons/README.md <==
# neurons

Live page: https://teoyujie.org/play/neurons/

A neuron explorer for SmolLM2-135M that runs in the web browser, written in Goose and compiled to WebAssembly.
The model runs on the GPU with WebGPU when the browser has a WebGPU adapter, and on the CPU in WebAssembly when it does not.
If the GPU cannot take the weights, the model runs on the CPU.
The page shows which path runs.

Inspired by [lyramakesmusic/neuron-explorer](https://github.com/lyramakesmusic/neuron-explorer).

The page shows the MLP neurons of [SmolLM2-135M](https://huggingface.co/HuggingFaceTB/SmolLM2-135M).
A sweep on a local GPU read all 46,080 neurons over 10 million tokens.
Claude labelled the neurons that stand out most in each layer.
The page ships that work as a snapshot, and it runs the model live:

- Run your own text, and see the strongest neurons at each token.
- Open a neuron, and see its top contexts, its label and its steering samples.
- Steer a neuron: set it to a value, and compare the text that the model writes with and without it.
- Compute the Jacobian lens of a neuron at a token of your text: the next tokens and the later neurons that it moves most.
- Swap a neuron between your text and another text, and compare the next-token probabilities.
- Ask Claude about a neuron with your own OpenRouter key.
- Share a link to a room, and explore with 1 to 4 other people at the same time.

## Run it

```sh
python3 -m http.server -d web 8000
# then open http://localhost:8000/
```

The repository has the built `web/engine.wasm` and the snapshot, so the page needs no build step.

## What the page downloads

| What | From | Size |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `config.json`, `tokenizer.json`, `model.safetensors` | `huggingface.co/HuggingFaceTB/SmolLM2-135M`, revision `93efa2f097d58c2a74874c7e644dbc9b0cee75a2` (`MODEL_URL` in `web/model.mjs`) | 269 MB, and 2 MB for the tokenizer |
| The page, the wasm and the snapshot | the server of the page | about 3 MB |

The page downloads the model only when you click "Load the model".
The worker keeps the files in the Cache API, so a second visit does not download them again.
The weights are bf16 in the file.
The worker converts them to f32 in WebAssembly memory (538 MB).
On the GPU path, the worker also copies them to GPU memory (538 MB).

## Ask Claude

This page cannot ask Claude for you, because a static page cannot keep an API key secret.
Thus each visitor puts an [OpenRouter](https://openrouter.ai/keys) key in the page.
The browser sends the key only to `openrouter.ai`, and keeps it in `sessionStorage` of that tab.
The key never goes to the server of the page or into this repository.
Claude gets the neuron's statistics, its top contexts and steering samples, the visitor's text and the last steering result.

## Rooms

The "Share a link" button makes a room and gives a link to it.
Each person who opens the link comes into the room, to a maximum of 5.
In a room, all members see the same view and the same notes:

- The view: the selected neuron, the text that ran, the selected token, and if the lens shows.
  Each member runs the model in their own tab.
  A member who did not load the model sees the neuron and the text only.
- The notes: one note on each neuron, to a maximum of 64 in a room.
  The page lists them under "Explore together".
- The members: the name of each member who sent a request in the last 15 seconds.

The rooms have no accounts.
The link is the key: the id of the room has 128 random bits, after `#room=` in the link.
The browser does not send the part after `#` in the URL of a request, so the page sends the id in the body of each request.
A room ends 3 days after it was made.
The relay keeps the rooms in memory only, and a restart of the relay ends all rooms.
The OpenRouter key never goes into a room (Ask Claude): the page sends only the fields of the protocol, and it does not send a request that holds the visitor's key or text like an OpenRouter key.
The relay also refuses a request that holds `sk-or-`.

The relay is `neurons_room`, a Goose daemon in the [server repository](https://github.com/yujieteo/server) (`room/`). httpd on teoyujie.org sends `POST /play/neurons/room` to it with FastCGI.
Thus `python3 -m http.server` gives the page without rooms.

### Transport

The page polls: one `POST` each second, with one request at a time. httpd in the OpenBSD base system cannot pass a WebSocket to a daemon, and a server-sent event stream or a long poll keeps one connection open for each member.
A poll is a short FastCGI request, so the relay serves one request at a time, with no thread and no lock.
A change comes to the other members in 1 to 2 seconds.

### Conflicts

- The view: the last view that comes to the relay wins.
  A view that the page did not send yet is newer than the view in a reply, so the page keeps its own view until the relay has it.
- A note: a save sends the version of the note that the member saw (`base`).
  If the note changed after that version, the relay does not change it, and the reply is 409 with the notes as they are now.
  The page then shows the other note, and keeps the member's text in the box.
  A second save replaces the other note.
- The page does not change a note that its member edits when the note of the room changes or when another neuron is selected.

### Protocol

Each request is `POST room` (next to the page) with a JSON body of at most 8,192 bytes:

| Field | In | Value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `op` | all | `create`, `poll`, `view`, `note` or `leave` |
| `room` | all | the id of the room: 22 characters of base64url |
| `member` | all | the id of the member in this tab: 8 to 16 characters of base64url, random |
| `name` | all | the name of the member: 1 to 32 bytes, no control characters |
| `since` | all | the `seq` of the last reply that had the view and the notes, or -1 |
| `view` | `view` | the view as a JSON string of an object, at most 2,048 bytes: `{"layer", "index", "text", "token", "lens"}` |
| `neuron`, `text`, `base` | `note` | the neuron as `layer.index`, the note (at most 500 bytes, empty to delete it), and the version of the note that the member saw (0 for no note) |

`create` makes the room.
Each other operation needs a room that exists.
Each request also marks its member as present.
The reply (200, and 409 for a note conflict) is the state of the room:

```json
{"seq": 7, "expires": 1793461540, "members": [{"name": "Ada", "you": true}, {"name": "Ben"}],
 "view": "{\"layer\":16,\"index\":1275,\"text\":\"The cat\",\"token\":1,\"lens\":false}", "view_by": "Ben",
 "notes": [{"neuron": "16.1275", "text": "Commas in lists", "by": "Ada", "version": 6}]}
```

`seq` goes up at each change of the view or a note.
`view`, `view_by` and `notes` come only when `since` is less than `seq`.
`expires` is the end of the room, in seconds since 1970.
The reply names no member id, so a member cannot speak as another.
An error has `{"error", "message"}`:

| Status | `error` | When |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 400 | `bad` | the request is not valid |
| 400 | `key` | the request holds `sk-or-` |
| 403 | `full` | the room has 5 members |
| 403 | `notes` | the room has 64 notes |
| 404 | `gone` | the room ended, or the id is wrong |
| 409 | `exists` | `create` for a room that exists |
| 413 | `big` | the body is more than 8,192 bytes |
| 429 | `slow` | a rate limit: 600 requests in a minute from one address, 6 new rooms in an hour from one address, 600 requests or 120 views and notes in a minute in one room |
| 503 | `rooms`, `busy` | the relay has 100 rooms, or counts for 4,096 addresses |

## Lens and swap

Both change one neuron at one token, and run the model again from that token.
They use the forward call of the engine and its steered neurons, so they run on the CPU path and on the GPU path.
`web/probes.mjs` has the two.

<img src="docs/figures/lens-swap.svg" width="520" alt="Two diagrams. a: a grid of tokens 1 to t by layers 0 to 29. The lens changes neuron (l, i) at token t to a plus or minus h, and reads the later neurons at t and the logits at t. b: your text has value a at token t, and the other text has value b at its last token. The swap gives b to your text and a to the other text, and compares the next-token probabilities before and after.">

The lens changes one neuron at one token, and reads only that token.
The swap exchanges the values of one neuron between two texts.

### Jacobian lens

The Jacobian lens of neuron (l, i) at token t of your text is the derivative, with respect to the value of the neuron at t, of:

- the logits at t (the next-token prediction), and
- the values of the neurons of layers l + 1 and after, at t.

The lens changes the neuron at t only, so its result is one column of the Jacobian of the forward pass at t.
The engine computes it by central differences: (f(a + h) - f(a - h)) / 2h, with a the neuron's value at t.
The step h is the neuron's standard deviation in the sweep.
On SmolLM2-135M, 5 neurons in layers 3 to 29 gave the same lens within 3e-3 of its largest value, for steps from 0.3 to 3 standard deviations.
Smaller steps gave larger errors, because of the f32 rounding of the logits.
The page shows the 8 tokens that the neuron raises most, the 8 that it lowers most, and the 10 later neurons that it moves most.

The idea comes from these sources:

- The logit lens reads the residual stream as next-token logits ([nostalgebraist, 2020](https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/AcKRB8wDpdaN6v6ru/interpreting-gpt-the-logit-lens)).
- An MLP neuron adds its output vector to the residual stream, and that vector promotes some tokens of the vocabulary ([Geva et al., 2022](https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.14680)).
  That is the direct effect only.
  The lens also includes the effect through the later layers.
- A gradient gives a linear estimate of the effect of a change of an activation ([Nanda, 2023, attribution patching](https://www.neelnanda.io/mechanistic-interpretability/attribution-patching)).

### Neuron swap

The neuron swap of neuron (l, i) between token t of your text and the last token of another text:

1. Runs each text, and reads the value of the neuron at its token: a in your text, b in the other text.
2. Runs your text again with the neuron set to b at t only, and the other text with the neuron set to a at its last token only.
3. Shows the next-token probabilities of each text before and after the swap.

This is an interchange intervention on one neuron ([Geiger et al., 2021](https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.02997)), the same as activation patching ([Vig et al., 2020](https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.12265); [Meng et al., 2022](https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.05262)) in the two directions.
Steering sets a neuron at every token.
The swap sets it at one token only, to a value that the model gave on real text.

The definitions in this section come from the sources above and from the design of this repository.

## How it works

<img src="docs/figures/dataflow.svg" width="520" alt="Block diagram. a: sweep.py reads 10 M tokens, explore.py picks 20 neurons in each layer and sends packs to Claude for labels, and writes web/snapshot. b: in the browser, the worker gets the weights from huggingface.co and runs the model on the GPU path (WebGPU) if the weights fit, else on the CPU path (WebAssembly). The page gets the wasm and the snapshot from its server, sends text to the worker, asks Claude through openrouter.ai, and polls the room relay.">

The sweep runs once on a local GPU and makes the snapshot.
In the browser, the worker runs the model, and the page talks to the worker, OpenRouter and the room relay.

A neuron is one element of `silu(gate_proj(x)) * up_proj(x)`, the input of `down_proj`, in each of the 30 layers.
There are 1,536 neurons in each layer.

| Part | File | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Engine | `engine/llama.goose` | The forward pass of a Llama-family model with tied embeddings: RMS norm, rotary embedding, grouped-query attention with a key-value cache, the SiLU MLP. It records the neuron values and sets steered neurons. |
| GPU path | `engine/gpu.c`, `engine/kernels.wgsl` | The same forward pass on WebGPU, through the WebGPU port of Emscripten (emdawnwebgpu), with WGSL kernels. The Goose engine checks each call and gives its tokens, steered neurons and buffers to `engine/gpu.c`. |
| Build | `build.sh` | Goose to C (`gen/llama.c`, `gen/llama.h`), then Emscripten to `web/engine.mjs` and `web/engine.wasm` from that C and `engine/gpu.c`. |
| Loader | `web/model.mjs` | Streams the safetensors file into WebAssembly memory, copies it to the GPU on the GPU path, and calls the engine. |
| Tokenizer | `web/tokenizer.mjs` | The byte-level BPE of SmolLM2. |
| Lens and swap | `web/probes.mjs` | The Jacobian lens and the neuron swap, on the forward call of the engine. |
| Rooms | `web/room.mjs` | The client of the room relay: the bodies of the requests, the key check, the polls, and the order of views and notes. |
| Worker | `web/worker.mjs` | Runs the model off the main thread. It takes the GPU path when `navigator.gpu` gives an adapter that takes the weights, else the CPU path. |
| Page | `web/index.html`, `web/app.js`, `web/art.mjs`, `web/style.css` | The look of [teoyujie.org](https://teoyujie.org): its shared stylesheet `https://teoyujie.org/shared/kowalski.css` and its art of random walks beside the normal density. |
| Sweep | `sweep/sweep.py`, `sweep/explore.py` | The local sweep, the choice of neurons, the label packs for Claude and the snapshot. |
| Snapshot | `web/snapshot/explored.json`, `web/snapshot/neurons.bin` | The explored neurons, and the statistics of all neurons. |

### Memory

WebAssembly has no address-space reservation, so Emscripten allocates each Goose data stack in full.
Thus the engine keeps its state in fixed-size globals, and the page keeps the weights in memory from `malloc`.
The page gives the weights to `forward` as a slice.
The only data-stack use is the zeroed temporary that `goose_init` builds for each global array.
The largest global array is `rec` (23.6 MB), so `build.sh` sets the stack to 32 MiB.

### Limits

- 128 tokens for each forward call, and 256 positions in the key-value cache.
- The page runs the first 128 tokens of your text, and makes 32 tokens when you steer.
- The capacities in `engine/llama.goose` fit SmolLM2-135M.
  Keep the engine to this model family: it has no upstream.

## Build

The build needs the Goose compiler at commit `a108677d300cb0e8bbb842c35a353abe67a20c6c`, with its standard library, and Emscripten.
On NixOS:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/aardappel/goose ../goose
git -C ../goose checkout a108677d300cb0e8bbb842c35a353abe67a20c6c
( cd ../goose && nix shell nixpkgs#cmake nixpkgs#clang nixpkgs#lld nixpkgs#gnumake --command \
    bash -c 'cmake -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++ && cmake --build build -j16' )
GOOSE_STDLIB=$PWD/../goose/stdlib nix shell nixpkgs#emscripten --command env GOOSE=$PWD/../goose/build/goose ./build.sh
```

The build used Emscripten 5.0.6.
On the first build, Emscripten downloads its WebGPU port (emdawnwebgpu).
`build.sh` writes `gen/sha256sums`, and the tests check the sums, so a stale C or wasm fails the tests.

## Tests

```sh
nix shell nixpkgs#nodejs --command npm test
nix shell nixpkgs#deno --command deno test --allow-read --allow-env test/engine.test.mjs
```

- `test/engine.test.mjs` runs a tiny random Llama model in the wasm engine and compares it with transformers in f32: the logits, the neuron values, the key-value cache, a steered neuron, the swap, and the Jacobian lens against the Jacobian from autograd (1e-3 of its largest value).
  `test/make_fixture.py` makes the model.
  Node has no WebGPU, so `npm test` runs the CPU path only.
  Deno has WebGPU, so `deno test` runs the CPU path and the GPU path.
  It also compares the two paths on the prompts of the fixture, unsteered and steered, and on the lens and the swap.
  With WebGPU but no adapter, the GPU tests fail.
  The tolerance between the two paths: for each compared tensor (the logits of a forward call, and its neuron values), max |gpu - cpu| <= 1e-5 x max |cpu|.
  The swap gives the logits of forward calls, so the same tolerance applies to them.
  The lens is a difference of two forward calls over 2h, so its tolerance is 1e-5 x max |cpu value| / h, with the logits or the neuron values without the change as the values.
  When `NEURONS_MODEL_DIR` names a directory with `config.json`, `tokenizer.json` and `model.safetensors` of SmolLM2-135M at the revision of `MODEL_URL` (`web/model.mjs`), `deno test` also compares the two paths on SmolLM2-135M: 6 prompts, each unsteered and with 2 steering cases.
  Without it, that test is skipped.
  In Deno, wgpu on an NVIDIA T1000 refuses a buffer of 64 MiB or more, so the SmolLM2-135M test ran on lavapipe (`VK_ICD_FILENAMES` set to the lavapipe ICD).
  Deno needs `libvulkan.so.1` on its library path to find a Vulkan adapter (on NixOS, from `nixpkgs#vulkan-loader`).
- `test/room.test.mjs` runs the room client against a fake relay: the fields of each body, the key check, the order of a view and a reply, and a note conflict.
  The tests of the relay itself are in the server repository.
- `test/tokenizer.test.mjs` compares the tokenizer with the Hugging Face tokenizer on 9 texts (`test/make_tokens.py`).
  It downloads `tokenizer.json` from Hugging Face.

CI runs `npm test`, and `deno test` on lavapipe (Mesa's Vulkan on the CPU), because the runner has no GPU.
Thus CI checks the WGSL kernels on one Vulkan implementation.
It does not run a real GPU, a browser or SmolLM2-135M.

On SmolLM2-135M, the largest difference from transformers was 3e-5 in the logits and 7e-5 in the neuron values.
In Node, one new token took 52 ms. At the tenth token of a text, the lens took 0.3 s and the swap 0.8 s.

The GPU path against the CPU path on SmolLM2-135M, in Chromium 156 on an NVIDIA T1000, over 6 prompts of up to 128 tokens with 3 steering cases each:

- The largest difference was 7e-5 in the logits (the largest logit was 29, so 2.4e-6 relative) and 6e-3 in the neuron values (the largest value was 2,626, so 2.3e-6 relative).
  Both are in the tolerance of 1e-5 relative.
- The greedy tokens were the same: 288 of 288, 16 after each prompt.
- One new token took 9 ms on the GPU path and 35 ms on the CPU path.
  A prompt with the neuron values recorded took 77 ms and 755 ms.

## Sweep

The sweep runs on a local GPU with PyTorch and transformers.
The model weights and the full sweep output stay out of the repository.

```sh
python sweep/sweep.py MODEL_DIR OUT 10000000 fineweb.parquet:0.7 cosmo.parquet:0.3
python sweep/explore.py pack MODEL_DIR OUT
# Claude labels the neurons of OUT/packs/*.txt into OUT/labels/*.json
python sweep/explore.py snapshot MODEL_DIR OUT web/snapshot
```

1. `sweep.py` runs the model over 128-token windows of the corpus.
   For each neuron, it keeps the mean, the standard deviation and the 32 largest values with their positions.
   It leaves out the first 4 positions of each window.
2. `explore.py pack` picks 20 neurons in each layer by how far their largest value stands above their mean, in units of their standard deviation.
   Of the 20, 5 are neurons whose top contexts share one or two tokens.
   Then it computes 8 top contexts for each neuron again, and the steering samples.
   It writes the label packs.
3. Claude reads each pack and writes a label and a description for each neuron.
4. `explore.py snapshot` writes the snapshot.

The corpus was the first shard of `fineweb-edu-dedup` (70 %) and of `cosmopedia-v2` (30 %) from [HuggingFaceTB/smollm-corpus](https://huggingface.co/datasets/HuggingFaceTB/smollm-corpus).

## Licence

MIT (see `LICENSE`), for the code of this repository.
SmolLM2-135M is Apache-2.0.
The repository does not include the weights.
Goose is Apache-2.0.
The generated `gen/llama.c` and `web/engine.wasm` contain the Goose runtime, so `web/GOOSE-LICENSE.txt` has the Goose licence.
The contexts in the snapshot are short extracts of FineWeb-Edu (ODC-By) and Cosmopedia (Apache-2.0).
==> ps-profile/README.md <==
# ps-profile

[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/github/license/yujieteo/ps-profile)](LICENSE) [![PowerShell 5.1](https://img.shields.io/badge/PowerShell-5.1-5391FE?logo=powershell&logoColor=white)](https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/scripting/windows-powershell/install/windows-powershell-system-requirements) [![Latest release](https://img.shields.io/github/v/release/yujieteo/ps-profile)](https://github.com/yujieteo/ps-profile/releases/latest)

A PowerShell profile for Windows PowerShell 5.1 on computers that use ConstrainedLanguage mode.
It adds commands to find files, search content, show files and move between folders.
It uses only cmdlets and core types.

## What it does

The profile adds these commands:

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `fd` | Find files by name. Use `-Ext`, `-Changed` (for example `30d`) and `-Size` (for example `+1MB`) to filter. Uses the file index for folders under `ShareRoots`. |
| `recent` | Show the 20 most recently changed files of the last 7 days under the current folder. Use `-Days` and `-Count` to change the limits. |
| `rg` (alias `grep`) | Search file content. Use `-Office` to search `.docx`, `.xlsx` and `.pptx` files. |
| `rgi` | Search file content and pick a match in the picker. The preview shows the lines around the match. The pick opens the file. |
| `fzf` | Pick an item from a list. Type text to filter the list. Type `#2` to pick item 2. |
| `br` | Browse a folder in the picker. The picker shows 2 levels. A folder pick enters the folder. A file pick opens the file. |
| `bat` | Show a file with line numbers. Office files show as text. Use `-Range 100-200` to show some lines and `-Pattern` to highlight text. |
| `du` | Show folder sizes as a tree, largest first. Use `-Depth` to change the number of levels. |
| `tldr` | Show examples for the profile commands. Run `tldr` with no argument to list the commands. |
| `z`, `zi` | Jump to a folder you used before. `zi` shows a picker. |
| `cd` | Change folder. Records the visit for `z`. |
| `delta` | Show the differences between two files. Office files are compared as text. |
| `ls`, `ll` | List files. Colors show the file type. |
| `cpath`, `unc`, `cb`, `pb` | Copy paths or text to the clipboard, or get the clipboard text. |
| `wc`, `head`, `tail`, `which`, `touch` | Small versions of common Unix commands. |
| `mkcd`, `up`, `..`, `...` | Create and enter a folder, or go to a parent folder. |
| `open` | Open a file with its default program. |
| `ep`, `reload` | Open the profile in Notepad. Load the profile again. |

## Picker and preview

The picker (`fzf`, `zi`, `rgi`, `br`, Ctrl+R and Ctrl+T) shows a preview at the side of the list.
The preview shows item 1 of the list:

- For a file, the preview shows the first lines with line numbers, as `bat` shows them.
  Office files show as text.
- For a folder, the preview shows the items in the folder.
- For an `rg` match, the preview shows the lines around the match.
  The match line is yellow.

The preview needs a window width of 60 characters or more.
The preview does not read files over 20 MB.

## Keys

| Key | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| Ctrl+R | Pick a command from the full PSReadLine history. The command goes to the clipboard. |
| Ctrl+T | Pick a file under the current folder. The path goes to the clipboard. |

After the pick, press Ctrl+V to insert the text on the command line.
ConstrainedLanguage mode blocks the PSReadLine methods that put text on the command line.
Thus the keys use the clipboard.

## Prompt

The prompt shows the current folder.
It also shows the time of commands that take more than 2 seconds.

## Install

1. Open Windows PowerShell and run `$PROFILE`.
   The output is the path of the profile file.
2. If the file does not exist, create it at that path.
3. Copy `Microsoft.PowerShell_profile.ps1` to that path.
   Replace the file if it exists.
4. Open a new Windows PowerShell window.

If PowerShell does not load the profile, run `Get-ExecutionPolicy` to check the execution policy.

## Share roots

The `fd` command uses a file index for network shares and mapped drives.
Set the folders to index in `$global:ShareRoots`, near the top of the profile:

```powershell
$global:ShareRoots = @('S:\Finance', '\\fileserver\public')
```

- Mapped drive letters are allowed.
  The profile converts them to the UNC path that Windows shows for the drive.
- Run `Update-Index` to build or refresh the index for all share roots.
- Run `Update-Index 'S:\Finance\2026'` to refresh one folder under a share root.
- At startup, the profile starts a background index job when the index is older than 24 hours.
  The prompt shows `[index building]` or `[index old]` when this applies.
- `fd` in a folder that is not under `ShareRoots` uses a recursive search instead.
- The index keeps the size and the change time of each file.
  Thus `fd -Changed`, `fd -Size` and `recent` are fast on share roots.
- The index builder lists a folder again only when the time of the folder changes.
  If a program changes a file but not its folder, the index keeps the old size and time of that file.
- Version 1.1.0 uses a new index format.
  After the upgrade, the profile builds the index of each share root again one time.
  The profile does not read an index of version 1.0.0, and it deletes the old files.

## Where the profile writes data

The profile writes its data to `$env:LOCALAPPDATA\ps-profile`.
The profile creates this folder at startup.
The folder contains:

- `idx2_*.idx` and `idx2_*.state`: the file index for each share root.
- `index.lock`: exists only while an index build runs.
- `idx2_*.partial`: exists while an index build is not finished.
  The index is `old` until the build ends.
- `z.txt`: the folder history for `z`.

When `rg -Office`, `bat`, `delta` or the preview reads an Office file, it unpacks the file in a temporary folder under `$env:TEMP`.
The profile deletes this folder after the search.

## ConstrainedLanguage limits

The profile does not use `Add-Type`, COM objects, or `Invoke-Expression`.
Because of these limits:

- The commands are PowerShell code.
  They do not call external programs such as `rg.exe` or `fzf.exe`.
- `ep` starts `notepad.exe`.
- `rg -Office`, `bat`, `delta` and the preview read Office files by unpacking the file and reading its XML.
  Legacy `.doc`, `.xls` and `.ppt` files are not read.
- `rg` skips binary files and files over 20 MB by default.
  Use `-MaxSizeMB` to change the limit.
- `rg` and `rgi` refuse a content search of a whole share.
  Give a folder instead.
- The picker uses `Read-Host`.
  Type text and press Enter to filter, or type `#` and the item number, for example `#12`, and press Enter to pick.
  A plain number filters the list.
  It does not filter on each key.
- Ctrl+R and Ctrl+T put the pick on the clipboard, not on the command line.
  See [Keys](#keys).

## Release

The current version is 1.2.0.
See [CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md).

After the change merges to the default branch:

1. Run `git tag v1.1.0` on the merged commit.
2. Run `git push origin v1.1.0`.
3. Create a GitHub release for the tag `v1.1.0`.
4. Attach `Microsoft.PowerShell_profile.ps1` to the release.

## License

This project is licensed under the MIT License.
See [LICENSE](LICENSE).
==> server/README.md <==
# teoyujie.org server

The config of the teoyujie.org server: OpenBSD 8.0 base system only, no packages.
It serves the site and the app pages, runs the pond save and read services and the room relay of the neuron explorer, forwards mail, and is a VPN with an ad blocker.

This repo is public and has no secret.
The server makes its own secrets (TLS key, ACME account key, SRS key, WireGuard key), and they never leave it.
`local.conf` (Git ignores it) has the private values.
No value in it gives access to the server.

## Files

| File | Use |
| --- | --- |
| `install.sh` | Runs on the server as root. Makes a fresh OpenBSD 8.0 server into teoyujie.org. Safe to run again. |
| `test.sh` | Runs on the server as root after `install.sh`. Tests all services. |
| `apply` | Runs on the Mac. Copies this repo and `local.conf` to the server, then runs `install.sh`. |
| `private-setup` | Runs on the Mac. The one-time setup of the private names of Lavish, femahoy and the fleet view (below). |
| `mail-alias` | Runs on the Mac. `./mail-alias add NAME` or `./mail-alias del NAME`. |
| `share` | Runs on the Mac. `./share FILE` prints a link to FILE that works for 30 days. |
| `local.conf.example` | The private values, with test values. CI uses it. |
| `etc/` | The configs: httpd, relayd, acme-client, smtpd, pf, sshd, unbound, doas, sysctl, crontab, and `rc.d/neurons_room`. |
| `bin/` | The jobs: health-check, ssh-alert, blocklist-update, site-update (below), private-cert (below), deploy-only (the forced command of the deploy user), tunnel-wait (the login shell of the tunnel user), and fleet-upload (the forced command of the fleet user, below). |
| `fleet/index.html` | The page of the fleet view (below). It shows the pages of the Mac and the desktop. |
| `www/` | Files on the web: security.txt, the GPG key with WKD, the MTA-STS policy, and `errdocs/err.html`, the error page (403, 404 and the others) in the look of the site. |
| `pond/` | The pond save and read services (below). `pond_save.c`, `pond_verify.h`, `pond_lock.h`, `pond_read.c` and `pond_read.h` are copies of `server/` in the [pond repo](https://github.com/yujieteo/pond) at commit `cb6bbac6ffcb050347d1fab6852e4302184d1362`. Do not change them here. `main.c` adds pledge and unveil to `pond_save`. `pond_read` does its own unveil (read only) and pledge (`stdio rpath`). |
| `room/` | The room relay of the neuron explorer (below). `room.c` is the C that Goose makes from `room.goose`. |
| `.githooks/pre-commit`, `.gitleaks.toml` | gitleaks stops a commit that has a secret in it. Turn on the hook once in each clone: `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`. |
| `.github/workflows/ci.yml` | gitleaks, and `install.sh` plus `test.sh` on a fresh OpenBSD 8.0 VM. |

## Rebuild from zero

1. At Vultr, deploy "OpenBSD 8.0" (plan vc2-1c-1gb, Singapore) with the SSH key of the Mac and the hostname `teoyujie.org`.
   Set the reverse DNS of the IPv4 and the IPv6 address to `teoyujie.org`.
2. On the Mac: `cp local.conf.example local.conf`, then put in the real values.
   Before the first apply, make the tunnels of the Mac and the iPhone in the WireGuard app (below) and put their public keys in `WG_PEERS`.
   After the first apply, SSH works only through the VPN.
3. `./apply root@NEW_IPV4`.
   It asks for the doas password of the admin and prints the WireGuard public key of the server.
   After this run, root cannot log in with SSH.
4. Put that key in the WireGuard configs of the Mac and the iPhone (below).
   Turn on the tunnel, then test: `ssh ADMIN_USER@10.8.0.1`.
   ADMIN_USER is the value in `local.conf`.
   The admin keys and the deploy key work only through the VPN.
   If the VPN breaks, log in as ADMIN_USER with the doas password in the Vultr web console.
5. In Gandi LiveDNS, set the records of the next section.
6. When `dig +short teoyujie.org` shows the new address: `ssh -t ADMIN_USER@10.8.0.1 'doas acme-client -v teoyujie.org && doas rcctl reload httpd && doas rcctl restart smtpd'`
7. `install.sh` deploys the site from the main branch of its repo (below).
   The site repo also has the app pages.
8. Test on the server: `ssh -t ADMIN_USER@10.8.0.1 doas sh server/test.sh`.
   Test from outside that the server is not an open relay.
   Expect 550: `python3 -c 'import smtplib; s=smtplib.SMTP("teoyujie.org"); s.ehlo(); s.mail("a@example.org"); print(s.rcpt("a@example.net"))'`

After a change to this repo: `./apply ADMIN_USER@10.8.0.1`.

## DNS records

| Name | Type | Value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `@` | A | the IPv4 address of the server |
| `@` | AAAA | the IPv6 address of the server |
| `@` | MX | `10 teoyujie.org.` |
| `@` | TXT | `"v=spf1 mx -all"` |
| `_dmarc` | TXT | `"v=DMARC1; p=reject"` |
| `www` | CNAME | `teoyujie.org.` |
| `mta-sts` | CNAME | `teoyujie.org.` |
| `_mta-sts` | TXT | `"v=STSv1; id=20261101"` |
| `_smtp._tls` | TXT | `"v=TLSRPTv1; rua=mailto:contact@teoyujie.org"` |
| `@` | CAA | `0 issue "letsencrypt.org"` |
| `@` | CAA | `0 issuewild ";"` |
| `@` | CAA | `0 iodef "mailto:contact@teoyujie.org"` |
| `mac.lavish` | A | the IPv4 address of the server |
| `desk.lavish` | A | the IPv4 address of the server |
| `fh` | A | the IPv4 address of the server |
| `fleet` | A | the IPv4 address of the server |

Make no other record.
A wildcard record stops GnuPG from finding the key with WKD.
If you change `www/.well-known/mta-sts.txt`, also change the `id` of `_mta-sts`.

## WireGuard clients

In the WireGuard app, add an empty tunnel.
The app makes the private key and shows the public key.
Put the public key in `WG_PEERS` in `local.conf`.
For the first apply, that is all (step 2 above).
To add a new device later, run `./apply ADMIN_USER@10.8.0.1` from a device that is already on the VPN.
If no device is on the VPN, log in to the Vultr web console.
Add the key to `local.conf` in the `server` folder of the last apply, then run its `install.sh` as root.

```
[Interface]
PrivateKey = (the app makes it)
Address = 10.8.0.2/32
DNS = 10.8.0.1

[Peer]
PublicKey = (the server key that install.sh prints)
Endpoint = teoyujie.org:51820
AllowedIPs = 10.8.0.0/24
PersistentKeepalive = 25
```

The iPhone uses `Address = 10.8.0.3/32`.
Only traffic to 10.8.0.0/24 goes through the VPN.
All DNS goes to the server, which blocks ads and trackers.
Turn on "On Demand" on both devices.
The iPhone reaches the Mac at 10.8.0.2 (Remote Login or Screen Sharing in macOS Settings).

## Site deploys

Each minute, the cron job `bin/site-update` (as the user deploy) reads the commit of the main branch of [yujieteo/site](https://github.com/yujieteo/site).
When it changes, the job gets the tarball of that commit from GitHub and gives its `public/` to deploy-only.
Then it gets `/` and each URL in `fixed-urls.txt` of that commit from httpd.
If one does not give 200, it puts the previous site back and mails root.
Thus a push to main deploys the site, with no key and no Mac.
`/home/deploy/site-commit` holds the last commit, so a failed commit is not deployed again.
The next commit on main deploys again.
If GitHub does not answer, the job tries again the next minute and mails nothing.

`scripts/deploy.sh` of the site repo also deploys from the Mac.
The next change on main replaces that deploy.

## Pond save and read services

`POST https://teoyujie.org/pond/entry` takes 1 signed change to 1 pond entry.
The pond repo has the protocol in `docs/protocol.md`. httpd sends `/pond/*` (but not `/pond/entries.json`) to `/var/www/cgi-bin/pond_save` through slowcgi.
The data is in `/var/www/pond`: `keys/` has the public keys of the devices, and `entries/` has the entries.
A deploy and `install.sh` do not change them.
The server has no private key.

To enrol a device, put the base64 of its public key (SubjectPublicKeyInfo DER) in `keys/NAME.pub`.
NAME is the `key` of its requests.
From the Mac:

```
scp NAME.pub ADMIN_USER@10.8.0.1:
ssh -t ADMIN_USER@10.8.0.1 doas install -m 644 NAME.pub /var/www/pond/keys/
```

To revoke the device, delete its file.

`GET /pond/entries.json`, `/log/` and `/log/feed.xml` show the entries, newest first. httpd sends them to `/var/www/cgi-bin/pond_read` through slowcgi.
`/log/` is the site file `log/template.html` with the entries in place of its line `<!-- pond:entries -->`.
Without that file, `/log/` is a plain page. pond_read can only read the entries and the template.

To use a new pond version, copy the 5 files from `server/` of the pond repo to `pond/`, change the commit in the table above, then apply.

## Room relay of the neuron explorer

`POST https://teoyujie.org/play/neurons/room` is the relay of the rooms of the [neuron explorer](https://github.com/yujieteo/neurons).
A share link opens a room, and 2 to 5 people see the same view and the same notes.
The README of the neurons repo has the protocol (Rooms). httpd sends the requests to `neurons_room`, a Goose daemon on 127.0.0.1 port 8790, with FastCGI.
`etc/rc.d/neurons_room` starts it as the user `_neuronsroom`.
It pledges `stdio inet`, so it cannot open a file.

- The page polls the relay once each second. httpd in the base system cannot pass a WebSocket to a daemon, and a server-sent event stream or a long poll keeps one connection open for each member.
  A poll is a short request, so the daemon serves one request at a time, with no thread and no lock.
- The daemon keeps the rooms in memory only.
  It writes no file and logs no room and no address.
  A room ends 3 days after it was made, and a restart of the daemon ends all rooms.
- The limits: 100 rooms, 5 members and 64 notes in a room, 8,192 bytes in a request, 2 s to send a request.
  In each minute, 600 requests from 1 address, and 600 requests or 120 views and notes in 1 room.
  In each hour, 6 new rooms from 1 address.
  For these limits, all of an IPv6 /64 is 1 address.
- Known limit: a room stays for 3 days, also with no members.
  Thus about 17 addresses can make all 100 rooms in 1 hour, and then nobody can make a room for up to 3 days.
- The daemon refuses a request that holds an OpenRouter key (`sk-or-v1-` and 64 hex digits) in a string of its JSON, so a key cannot go into a room.

To change the relay, change `room.goose`, then make `room.c` again with Goose at commit `a108677d300cb0e8bbb842c35a353abe67a20c6c` (its build is in the README of the neurons repo):

```
cd room && GOOSE_STDLIB=../../goose/stdlib ../../goose/build/goose --standalone --include room_net.h -o room.c room.goose
```

`install.sh` builds the daemon from `room.c`.
`test.sh` tests it through httpd, and the limits on 2 more daemons.

## Private names of Lavish and femahoy

On a device on the VPN (Mac, iPhone, desktop), these names open the apps.
No token is necessary.

| Name | App | Tunnel port on 10.8.0.1 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `https://mac.lavish.teoyujie.org` | Lavish on the Mac (127.0.0.1:4387) | 4387 |
| `https://desk.lavish.teoyujie.org` | Lavish on the desktop (127.0.0.1:4387) | 4388 |
| `https://fh.teoyujie.org` | femahoy `fh serve` on the desktop (127.0.0.1:8790) | 8790 |

Lavish uses paths from the root of the site (`/api/...`, `/events/...`), so each Lavish has its own name, not a path such as `/mac`.
Nobody without a WireGuard key gets to them.

<img src="docs/figures/private-names.svg" width="360" alt="The path of a request to a private name. A VPN device asks unbound, which gives the name as 10.8.0.1. The device sends HTTPS to relayd on 10.8.0.1 port 443, which gives 403 to a cross-site Origin. relayd sends mac.lavish to port 4387, desk.lavish to port 4388, fh to port 8790 and fleet to httpd port 8092. Reverse SSH tunnels take ports 4387, 4388 and 8790 to Lavish on the Mac, Lavish on the desktop and femahoy on the desktop. From the internet, the public httpd answers only the Let's Encrypt check for these names.">

*Only a VPN device reaches relayd. relayd sends each name to its tunnel port or to the fleet view.*

- The Mac and the desktop open a reverse SSH tunnel to the server, as the user `tunnel`.
  The tunnel ports listen only on 10.8.0.1, the VPN address of the server.
  The room relay also uses port 8790, but on 127.0.0.1, so the two do not collide.
- The user `tunnel` can only open these 3 tunnels (`Match User tunnel` in `etc/sshd_config`).
  It cannot run a command, get a terminal, or connect from the server to another address.
  It logs in with a key only, and only through the VPN.
- unbound gives the 3 names to the VPN devices as 10.8.0.1. relayd on 10.8.0.1 port 443 (`etc/relayd.conf`) sends each name to its tunnel. relayd gives 403 to a request with an `Origin` other than its own name, or with `Sec-Fetch-Site: cross-site`, so a web page cannot use the apps.
  The 403 page has the look of the site error page.
  Then relayd sets `Host` and `Origin` to the local address of the app.
  For femahoy, relayd also sends the token of `fh serve` (`FH_TOKEN` in `local.conf`). httpd on 10.8.0.1 port 80 sends the names to HTTPS.
- pf lets the internet in only to the public addresses.
  On the public addresses, httpd answers only the Let's Encrypt challenge for the 3 names.
  `bin/private-cert` gets their certificate.

To set it up:

1. In Gandi LiveDNS, add the records `mac.lavish`, `desk.lavish` and `fh` of the table in [DNS records](#dns-records).
   This is the only step by hand.
   Let's Encrypt needs them to check the names.
   The records give the public address, which answers only the check.
2. On the Mac (also from Termius over SSH), with the VPN on: `./private-setup ADMIN_USER@10.8.0.1`.
   It makes the key `~/.ssh/tunnel_ed25519` on the Mac and on the desktop (the private keys stay there).
   It puts the public keys (`TUNNEL_KEY`) and the femahoy token (`FH_TOKEN`) in `local.conf`, runs `apply` (it asks for the doas password), and runs `rebuild` on the Mac and on the desktop.
   The dotfiles make the tunnel services.
   If a step fails, the command says which step and how to try again.
   It is safe to run again, for example after a new femahoy token.

`test.sh` tests the tunnel user, the listen addresses, the pf rules, the headers that relayd sends to each app, and that the public addresses do not answer for the 3 names.

## Fleet view

`https://fleet.teoyujie.org` shows the workers on the Mac and on the desktop: each worker, its task, its last event, and each PR that waits for a word from the captain.
It is read only, and only for the VPN devices.
The Mac and the desktop make their pages (the dotfiles do that, not this repo) and upload them:

```
ssh -i ~/.ssh/tunnel_ed25519 fleet@10.8.0.1 mac < mac.html        # on the Mac
ssh -i ~/.ssh/tunnel_ed25519 fleet@10.8.0.1 desk < desk.html      # on the desktop
```

- The user `fleet` logs in with the tunnel keys (`TUNNEL_KEY` in `local.conf`), only through the VPN.
  It can only run `bin/fleet-upload`: no shell, no terminal, no forwarding.
  Root owns its home.
- `fleet-upload` accepts only the name `mac` or `desk`, and a page of 1 MiB or less.
  It writes the page to a temp file in `/var/www/fleet`, then renames it to `mac.html` or `desk.html`.
  Thus httpd always serves a complete page, and a failed upload keeps the old page.
- `fleet/index.html` shows the 2 pages and the time of each upload (the `Last-Modified` of the page).
  It says OLD when an upload is more than 10 minutes ago.
  It loads the pages again each 30 seconds.
  The pages show in a sandbox, so they cannot run a script.
  Use HTML and CSS only.
- unbound gives `fleet.teoyujie.org` to the VPN devices as 10.8.0.1. relayd on 10.8.0.1 port 443 sends it to httpd on 127.0.0.1 port 8092, with the same `Origin` and `Sec-Fetch-Site` rule as the other private names.
  The public addresses answer only the Let's Encrypt challenge for it, as for the other private names.
  To set it up, add the DNS record `fleet` (in [DNS records](#dns-records)), then `./apply ADMIN_USER@10.8.0.1`.
  Until the public DNS has that record, `bin/private-cert` renews no private name.
  If the tunnel keys are not set up yet, run `./private-setup` (below) in place of `./apply`.

`test.sh` tests the names, the size limit, the replace in 1 step, and that the public addresses do not answer.

## Upgrade to the next OpenBSD release (each 6 months)

```
ssh -t ADMIN_USER@10.8.0.1 doas sysupgrade      # downloads, reboots, upgrades: about 10 minutes
./apply ADMIN_USER@10.8.0.1                     # writes the configs of this repo again
ssh -t ADMIN_USER@10.8.0.1 doas sh server/test.sh
```

After the upgrade, sysmerge mails root a list of the configs that it did not change.
`apply` writes the repo version of each of them, so you do not need to merge them by hand.

Change `8.0` in this file and in `.github/workflows/ci.yml` to the new release.
==> site/AGENTS.md <==
# Agents

1. Read REQUIREMENTS.md.
   Keep the site to what it says.
   Prefer deleting to adding.
2. Edit pages in `public/` by hand.
   No generator, no framework, no build step.
   The beam page and the wallpaper module (`scripts/build-wallpaper.sh`) are the exceptions.
3. Never edit `public/play/beamdiag/index.html`.
   Change the beam in `beamdiag/`, then run `scripts/build-beam.sh` and commit the page with the source.
4. Never change or remove a URL in `fixed-urls.txt`: the App Store links to them.
5. Commit to `main`.
   Turn on the VPN, then run `scripts/deploy.sh`.
   It runs the check, deploys and tests the URLs.
==> site/README.md <==
# site

This is the personal website of Teo Yu Jie, at https://teoyujie.org/.
It holds a home page of selected explorations, the beam diagram tool, the privacy and support pages of Lullapixie and Marklove, the OpenPGP key of contact@teoyujie.org, and the template of the log at `/log`.
`REQUIREMENTS.md` says what the site must do.

| Path | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| `public/` | Everything the server serves, as it is served. |
| `beamdiag/` | The beam diagram creator, a Goose program that builds to WebAssembly. `beamdiag/REQUIREMENTS.md` is its contract. |
| `wallpaper/` | The wallpaper maker, a Goose program (`wallpaper.goose`) with a C host (`host.c`) that builds to WebAssembly. `wasm.sha256` is the hash of its sources. |
| `public/fonts/` | The site fonts, Inter (Google Fonts v20, variable) and IBM Plex Mono (weight 400), with their OFL licences. |
| `public/shared/kowalski.css` | The tokens and interaction rules of the site look. `public/style.css` imports it. |
| `public/play/beamdiag/` | The built beam page. `index.html` and `beamdiag.wasm.gz` come from `scripts/build-beam.sh`. Never edit them by hand. |
| `public/play/wallpaper/` | The wallpaper page. `wallpaper.wasm.gz` comes from `scripts/build-wallpaper.sh`. Never edit it by hand. |
| `media-plan.md` | The master copy of the media plan. The pond app edits it. |
| `scripts/build-beam.sh [folder]` | Builds the beam page into `public/play/beamdiag/` or into the folder. |
| `scripts/build-wallpaper.sh` | Builds the wallpaper module with `goose` and `emcc` and writes `wallpaper/wasm.sha256`. |
| `scripts/check.sh [--replay [--accept]]` | Installs from `bun.lock`, then runs `tsc --noEmit` (strict) and `knip` over the TypeScript, then `scripts/check-site.ts`: pages and WKD key files exist, links resolve, the home page keeps its links and each selected exploration has a kind, a title and a link, the log template has one entries slot, each essay figure has a caption and alt text, the home page and beam page first loads are inside their gzipped budgets (100 KB and 590 KB), TypeScript has no comment without an `https://` source and no `any`, non-null assertion, cast or type argument on a DOM query. It also checks that `scripts/*.sh`, `wallpaper/` and the `beamdiag/` sources have no comments (source credits excepted), that `beamdiag/` builds the committed beam page, that the wallpaper module matches its sources, and that `bun test` and `beamdiag/test.goose` (built with ASan and UBSan) pass. It needs `bun`, `node`, `cc` and the beam build tools. It stops if `beamdiag/c/test.sha256` does not match the test inputs: then set `GOOSE` to make `beamdiag/c/test.c` again. With `--replay` it also captures the inputs of `bun test` and replays them on `main` and on `HEAD` (`scripts/replay.ts`); it fails on any output difference unless `--accept` is given. |
| `scripts/deploy.sh` | Runs the check, sends `public/` as a tar archive to deploy@10.8.0.1 over the VPN, then tests the URLs. |

The beam build needs `emcc`, `cc` and `gzip`, and `bun` 1.4.4 when `GOOSE` is set.
It uses the Goose C committed in `beamdiag/c/`.
It stops if `beamdiag/c.sha256` does not match the Goose inputs: then set `GOOSE` to make the C again.

The wallpaper build needs `goose` and `emcc`.
Set `GOOSE` and `EMCC` to use other binaries.

The site before 2026-10-18 (notebooks, stories, Play tools, the engine) is at the tag `old-site`.
==> skein/README.md <==
# Skein

Skein is a typed, units-checked language for numerical simulation.
It compiles to [Goose](https://aardappel.github.io/goose/), which generates C for native executables.
`skein build --target wasm` writes a page that meshes and solves a model in the browser, with C from Goose compiled by `emcc`.

```skein
model cantilever: 3d, mm_N_MPa_s

scales 40 mm, 2.5 MPa

let beam: Solid = box((0, 0, 0) mm, (200, 20, 40) mm)
let root: Surface = boundary(beam, x == 0 mm)
let tip: Surface = boundary(beam, x == 200 mm)

material steel {
    E: Pressure = 210 GPa
    nu: Real = 0.3
}

mesh m on beam {
    size: 5 mm
}

physics solid on beam solves u: Vector<Length> {
    material steel
    assume small_strain, isotropic
    let sigma: Tensor2<Pressure> = hooke(E, nu, strain(u))
    eq div(sigma) = 0
}

step load: static {
    bc root: u = 0
    bc tip: sigma*n = (0, 0, -2.5) MPa
    output u, von_mises(sigma)
}
```

**Status:** the compiler has a front end and native solvers for a limited set of models.
`skein check` parses and type-checks a model.
`skein lint` checks it and reports lint diagnostics.
With `--mesh`, it also applies the rules that need a mesh.
`skein verify` creates the mesh and checks its quality.
`skein verify convergence` solves an `fv` or `dg` model on finer meshes and checks the order of convergence of each `expect` of two fields.
`skein md` writes a model as a Markdown document.
`skein fmt` rewrites a file in the standard layout.
`skein export` writes the surface of a shape as STL or as tessellated STEP AP242.
`skein run --study` runs each case of a `grid`, `zip` or `sample` study on parallel workers, and prints the table of the cases.
`skein
report` writes one HTML file with the source, the solver tables and a WebGL viewer of the results of each case.
`skein build --target wasm` writes one HTML page with a control for each `param` with a range: the page meshes and solves the model again in the browser for each value.
The [command reference](docs/command_line.md) states the current limits.
The [roadmap](docs/roadmap.md) gives the planned phases.

## Building

The compiler is written in [Goose](https://aardappel.github.io/goose/).
Put `goose` on `PATH`, or set `GOOSE` to its path.
If `goose` cannot find its standard library, set `GOOSE_STDLIB` to the `stdlib` directory of a Goose source tree at the same commit as the compiler.

```
./build.sh          # writes build/skein
./build.sh test     # builds, then runs each src/<part>/test.goose and checks the generated C
```

The C that Goose generates from each model that `skein run` can run is in the repository next to the model, for audit.
For example, `examples/femahoy/grid_cantilever.c` is the C of `examples/femahoy/grid_cantilever.skn`.
`./build.sh test` runs each of these models and fails if the C differs.
It needs the Goose version in `goose_version` in `scripts/generated_c.sh`.
After a change to the code generator, the runtime or Goose, write the C again:

```
./build.sh && scripts/generated_c.sh write
```

The list of these models is in `scripts/generated_c.sh`.

The test of `src/md/` compares the documents of two models with the golden files in `tests/md/`.
After a change to `skein md`, write the golden files again and read the diff:

```
./build.sh
./build/skein md samples/01_cantilever.skn > tests/md/01_cantilever.md
./build/skein md examples/femahoy/heat_fin.skn > tests/md/heat_fin.md
```

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `src/skein.goose` | command dispatch |
| `src/syntax/` | syntax tree, lexer, parser and library files |
| `src/check/` | type and unit checks, lint rules and parameter dependencies |
| `src/fmt/` | source layout |
| `src/cli/` | language card for `skein help --llm` |
| `src/md/` | model documents |
| `src/codegen/` | native code generation and study execution |
| `src/runtime/` | hexahedron meshes, solid elements, solvers and VTU output |
| `src/solvers/` | sparse matrices, ILU(0), GMRES, a banded LU solve and Newton |
| `src/elements/` | triangle and tetrahedron elements for elasticity, heat and the complex wave equation of `harmonic` steps, and the Nédélec and Raviart-Thomas elements |
| `src/mesher/` | geometry meshes, mesh checks, and STL and STEP export |
| `src/shells/` | shell, beam and laminate solver routines, plus code generation for cylindrical shells |
| `src/transient/` | transient heat solver routines |
| `src/flow/` | stabilized Stokes and Navier-Stokes solver routines with temperature coupling, plus code generation for 2D flow |
| `src/cons/` | `fv` systems of conservation laws on triangles (the Euler equations), with a MUSCL limiter, a Rusanov flux and `ssprk3` |
| `src/meshless/` | point clouds, `rkpm` and `gfd` for one scalar unknown and 2D elasticity, and their direct solve |
| `src/optim/` | a Q4 grid of 2D elasticity with a band Cholesky solve, the adjoint of a point objective, the `topology` loop (SIMP, the sensitivity filter and the optimality criteria update), and the gradients of `minimize` |

## Why Skein

Abaqus, APDL and NASTRAN decks are hard to write and hard to check.
Their units are a convention that the user must remember.
Their physics is a fixed element library.
Their errors appear late, in the solver.

The [command reference](docs/command_line.md) states which tools exist now.
The language design has these goals:

- **Physics in strong form.**
  You write `div(sigma) = 0` and the constitutive law, as in a textbook.
  The compiler derives the weak form, the finite-volume balance or the meshless equations.
  A standard library of physics templates, in Skein source, covers the common physics.
- **Named modelling assumptions.**
  `assume small_strain, plane_stress(2 mm)` has an exact meaning.
  The checker rejects assumptions that conflict.
- **Units in the type system.**
  `E: Pressure = 210 GPa`.
  The checker rejects `E + rho` before any solve.
- **One language for the whole model.**
  Geometry, mesh, physics, method, steps and checks are in one file.
  Skein does not import meshes.
  Its mesher runs in the browser too.
- **Many methods, one physics.**
  Continuous and discontinuous Galerkin, finite volume, and meshless methods (RKPM, generalized finite differences).
- **Advanced analysis from the start.**
  Element birth and death, submodels, CFD stabilization, coupled physics, modal and buckling steps.
- **Verification by default.**
  Each run checks the rigid-body modes, the mesh quality, the residual, the equilibrium, the energy balance and the conservation of mass, and prints the result.
- **Written for LLMs, reviewed by people.**
  Rust-like declarations, every type explicit, types only at declarations, compact TOON tables and output, and stable diagnostic ids with a fix.
  One item on each line and no column alignment, so a small change gives a small diff.
- **Dimensions done right.**
  Each model has a `scales` line, and the solver works in scaled variables.
  The planned `skein check --groups` command prints `Re`, `Pr` and the other groups of the model.
  A `param` and a `study` sweep a model, with the cases on parallel workers.

Simulation is the core.
Parametric parts and export come later, and the [tradeoffs](docs/tradeoffs.md) rank all the goals.

## Documentation

- [Documentation index](docs/index.md)
- [Tutorial](docs/tutorial.md)
- [Language reference](docs/reference.md)
- [Specification](docs/skein_spec.md)
- [Command line](docs/command_line.md)
- [Checks](docs/checks.md)
- [Samples](samples/README.md)
- [Roadmap](docs/roadmap.md)
- [Design decisions](docs/decisions.md)
- [Tradeoffs](docs/tradeoffs.md)
- [Grilling questions and answers](docs/grilling.md)
- [The LLM test](docs/llm_test.md)

## License

Apache 2.0.
See [LICENSE](LICENSE).
