==> skills/concept-ui/references/design-audit.md <==
# Website Design Audit Standard

30 Oct 2026 · Teo Yu Jie

Each rule has an ID, a pass test, and a source.
Audit one page at a time.
Mark each rule Pass, Fail, or N/A.
A rule marked Fail needs a fix or a written reason.

## 1. Motion

| ID | Rule | Pass test | Source |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| M1 | Each animation has a purpose: explain a feature, give feedback, keep spatial sense, or (seldom seen) give delight. | Write the purpose for each animation. Remove an animation with no purpose. | Emil: You Don't Need Animations |
| M2 | Do not animate actions that users do tens or hundreds of times each day (command menu open, list hover). Never animate keyboard actions. | Arrow-key highlight in a list has transition-duration 0. A daily menu opens with no animation. | Emil: You Don't Need Animations |
| M3 | UI animation takes less than 300 ms. Micro-interaction: 100-150 ms. Tooltip, dropdown: 150-250 ms. Modal, drawer: 200-300 ms. Exit is about 20% faster than entrance. A larger element is slower than a small one. | List every duration in the CSS. Each value is in range. Allowed exceptions: marketing pages, a bottom sheet at 500 ms, and page-load or page-transition motion up to 600 ms. Observed: paco.me enter animation 0.6 s; shud.in page transition 0.4 s and 0.6 s. | Emil: Agents with Taste; Building a Drawer |
| M4 | Easing: element enters or leaves the screen = ease-out. Element moves on screen = ease-in-out. Hover change = ease. Constant motion = linear. Default = ease-out. | No ease-in on any UI transition. Observed: hover and color changes on emilkowal.ski and shud.in use cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1) at 0.15 s (Tailwind default). This is allowed. | Emil: 7 Practical Tips; Agents with Taste |
| M5 | Use custom curves, not the built-in ones, for important motion. Reference curves: sheet = cubic-bezier(0.32, 0.72, 0, 1). Reveal = cubic-bezier(0.77, 0, 0.175, 1). | Curves are named tokens in one file. | Emil: Drawer; Clip Path |
| M6 | A pressed button scales down to a value from 0.94 to 0.97. | Each button has transform: scale(0.94) to scale(0.97) on :active. Observed: emilkowal.ski uses 0.94 and 0.96. | Emil: 7 Practical Tips |
| M7 | An element that appears starts at scale 0.9 or more (0.93-0.95 in the examples). Never start at scale(0). | No scale(0) in any enter animation. | Emil: 7 Practical Tips |
| M8 | A popover scales from its trigger. | transform-origin is set from the trigger (Radix: --radix-dropdown-menu-content-transform-origin; Base UI: --transform-origin). It is not center. | Emil: 7 Practical Tips |
| M9 | First tooltip waits before it opens. The next tooltip opens at once, with no delay and no animation. Tooltip transition: 0.125 s ease-out on opacity and transform, from scale 0.97. | Move between two tooltips: the second has transition-duration 0ms. | Emil: 7 Practical Tips |
| M10 | Use CSS transitions, not keyframes, for state that can change while it moves (stacks, toggles). Use @starting-style for enter. | A fast second trigger does not make an element jump. | Emil: Toast |
| M11 | If a crossfade still looks wrong after you tune easing and duration, add filter: blur() under 20 px (2 px in the example). Use this last. | Each blur value is under 20 px and has a note that says why. | Emil: 7 Practical Tips; Agents with Taste |
| M12 | Animate transform, opacity, clip-path, and small blur only. Do not animate width, height, or margin. Shaky motion: add will-change: transform. Hover flicker: animate the child, not the parent. Do not use translateZ(0) as a hack. | Search the CSS for transitions on layout properties. None found. | Emil: Clip Path; Agents with Taste. bendc: Guidelines |
| M13 | During a drag, set transform on the element itself. Do not change a CSS variable on a parent with many children. | Drag a 20+ item drawer. No dropped frames. | Emil: Drawer |
| M14 | A reveal on scroll runs one time and starts when 100 px or more of the element is in view. Use IntersectionObserver or a hook with the same behavior. Reveal: clip-path inset(0 0 100% 0) to inset(0), 1 s. | Scroll to the element. It animates once. No layout shift. | Emil: Clip Path |
| M15 | Page-load animation and view transitions run only when the user has not asked for reduced motion. | Set prefers-reduced-motion: reduce. No enter animation and no view transition runs. | Observed: paco.me (no-preference gate), shud.in (not reduce gate), emilkowal.ski (motion-reduce classes) |

## 2. Components

| ID | Rule | Pass test | Source |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| K1 | Toast: it closes after 4 s. The timer stops on hover and when the browser tab is hidden (document.hidden). | Hover a toast, then switch tab. The toast stays. | Emil: Toast |
| K2 | Toast stack: each toast is offset by gap x index, and scaled by 1 - 0.05 x index. In stacked mode, all toasts have the height of the front toast. Hover expands the stack. | Add 3 toasts with different heights. The edges line up. | Emil: Toast |
| K3 | Fill the gaps between stacked items with an :after pseudo-element, so hover does not break. | Move the pointer slowly across a gap. The stack stays open. | Emil: Toast |
| K4 | Swipe to dismiss uses speed, not only distance. Speed = distance / time. Dismiss when distance passes the limit or speed is above 0.11 (value from Sonner, found by trial). | A short fast flick closes the toast. | Emil: Toast |
| K5 | A drag that goes the wrong way gets friction: it slows and stops. It does not stop at once. The element keeps pointer capture while it drags. | Drag outside the element. The drag continues. | Emil: Toast; Drawer |
| K6 | Sheet or drawer motion: transform 0.5 s cubic-bezier(0.32, 0.72, 0, 1). | The CSS has this exact transition. | Emil: Drawer |
| K7 | A drawer starts a drag only when its content is scrolled to the top. After a scroll reaches the top, block dragging for 100 ms. Ignore a second finger until the first one lifts. | Fast scroll to the top does not close the drawer. | Emil: Drawer |
| K8 | Drawer snap points are a fraction of the viewport or a fixed px value. On release, go to the nearest point. A hard flick can skip points. | Release at 3 positions. Each one snaps to the right point. | Emil: Drawer |
| K9 | When the keyboard opens, use the Visual Viewport API to set drawer height and bottom position. | Focus an input on a phone. The input and drawer content stay visible. | Emil: Drawer |
| K10 | Build overlays on an accessible primitive (Radix Dialog, Popover, Tabs). It handles focus and ARIA. | The overlay traps focus and closes with Escape. | Emil: Drawer; Clip Path. Paco: cmdk |
| K11 | Command menu: the page does not listen for the open key by default. Add the Cmd+K and Ctrl+K listener yourself. Escape, or Backspace on an empty field, goes back one level. | Keyboard-only use works for open, search, select, go back. | Paco: cmdk |
| K12 | Command list: height follows content with transition: height 100ms ease (--cmdk-list-height). Set scroll-padding-block to 8px at the start and end. | The list does not jump when results change. | Paco: cmdk |
| K13 | Command menu has a visible empty state and a loading state. It has no virtualization, so keep it under about 2,000-3,000 items. | Search for a string with no match. A message shows. | Paco: cmdk |
| K14 | Tab highlight: copy the tab list, style the copy as active, and clip it with clip-path: inset(0 R% 0 L% round 17px). Animate the clip to the new tab. The copy has aria-hidden and tabindex -1. | The highlight moves with no color-fade lag. | Emil: Clip Path (technique from Paco) |

## 3. Type

| ID | Rule | Pass test | Source |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| T1 | Load Inter with a preconnect and the stylesheet. Set :root font-family: Inter, sans-serif and font-feature-settings: 'liga' 1, 'calt' 1. Inside @supports (font-variation-settings: normal), use InterVariable, sans-serif. | The computed font of body text is Inter or InterVariable. @font-face has font-display: swap. Observed: rsms.me. | rsms: Inter |
| T2 | Numbers in tables, prices, and counters use tabular figures (tnum, or font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums). | Digits in a column have equal width. | rsms: Inter; Emil: Agents with Taste |
| T3 | Use the slashed zero (zero) or the disambiguation set (ss02) only where 0/O or I/l can be confused, for example codes and IDs. | Each use is in an ID or code context. | rsms: Inter |
| T4 | The prose column is 692 px wide at most. | max-width on prose is 692 px or less. Observed: paco.me 640 px, shud.in 672 px, emilkowal.ski 692 px. The 65ch value is in Emil's skill, but his own site is wider. | Emil: Agents with Taste |
| T5 | Headings use text-wrap: balance. No heading ends with one word on its last line. | Resize from 320 px to 1440 px. No orphan word in any heading. Observed: shud.in h1 has text-wrap: balance. | Shu: react-wrap-balancer |
| T6 | Use the single character …, not three dots. | Search the markup for "...". None in UI text. | Emil: Agents with Taste |
| T7 | Uppercase labels have wider letter-spacing. | Each uppercase label has letter-spacing above 0. | Emil: Agents with Taste |
| T8 | The fallback font stack has x-height and weight close to the main font. | Block the font file. The layout shift is small. | Emil: Agents with Taste |
| T9 | Underline is for links only. Use weight or color for other emphasis. Use bold for UI emphasis. Use italic only for citations and stress in prose. | No underlined text that is not a link. | Emil: Agents with Taste |
| T10 | Line height is unitless (1.5 to 1.7 for body text; observed 1.5 on emilkowal.ski and rsms.me, 1.71 on shud.in). Relative sizes use rem. | No px line-height. No em font-size. | bendc: Guidelines |
| T11 | Set one fixed group of stylistic font features on body, for example cv01 and ss03. | The computed font-feature-settings of body lists the group. Observed: emilkowal.ski (cv01, ss03), shud.in (calt, cv01, cv03, cv04, liga, ss03). | Observed: emilkowal.ski, shud.in |

## 4. Layout and code

| ID | Rule | Pass test | Source |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| L1 | Use semantic HTML: main, article, header, time with datetime. Use a real a or button for each link and button. | No div or span with a click handler and a button look. | bendc: Guidelines |
| L2 | Alt text on each image. A label on each form control. Do not use color alone to give information. | Run an accessibility check. Zero missing alt or label errors. | bendc: Guidelines |
| L3 | The html tag has lang. The document declares UTF-8 (meta charset). | Both are in the page source. | bendc: Guidelines |
| L4 | Scripts do not block the first render. Put them after the content. | No render-blocking script in the head. | bendc: Guidelines |
| L5 | One global box-sizing: border-box. Do not change the box model on single elements. | One box-sizing rule in the CSS. | bendc: Guidelines |
| L6 | Keep elements in normal flow. Use flex or grid. Use position: absolute only for stacked or layered components (for example a toast stack). | Each position: absolute has a written reason. | bendc: Guidelines; Emil: Toast |
| L7 | A selector has at most 3 structural parts. No id selectors. No !important. Do not override a style when a better selector avoids it. | Run a CSS linter with these limits. | bendc: Guidelines |
| L8 | A small control has a hit area of at least 44 px. Make it larger with a pseudo-element. | Measure each icon button. 44 px or more in both directions. | Emil: Agents with Taste |
| L9 | Give feedback at once for each user action: a loading state on submit, a success state on copy. | Click each action. A visible state change shows at once. | Emil: 7 Practical Tips |
| L10 | Test worst-case data: long names, empty lists, large counts. | Each list and card holds an empty case and a long-text case. | Emil: skill list (break-ui) |

## 5. Theme

| ID | Rule | Pass test | Source |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| C1 | The default theme is the system setting (prefers-color-scheme). The user can also pick light or dark. | Change the OS theme. The page follows. A site with one theme says so with `<meta name="color-scheme" content="only light">` (observed: shud.in). A site can follow the system with no toggle (observed: paco.me). | Paco: next-themes |
| C2 | No flash on load. A small script sets the theme attribute on the html tag before first paint. | Hard reload in dark mode with a production build. No light frame shows. | Paco: next-themes |
| C3 | Set color-scheme so built-in controls (inputs, buttons, scrollbars) match the theme. | A native input is dark in dark mode. | Paco: next-themes |
| C4 | Colors are CSS variables on :root. The dark values are under [data-theme='dark'] on the html tag. | No hard-coded color in a component. | Paco: next-themes |
| C5 | Switch the theme with all transitions off for one moment, then turn them on again. | During a switch, all elements change at the same time. | Paco: next-themes |
| C6 | A theme change in one tab changes all open tabs. | Open 2 tabs. Switch in one. The other follows. | Paco: next-themes |
| C7 | The toggle shows "System" when the system setting is active. It renders only after mount, with a placeholder of the same size. | No hydration warning. No layout shift. | Paco: next-themes |
| C8 | An image that depends on the theme: render both images and hide one with CSS, or use an empty placeholder until the theme is known. | No wrong-theme image flash. | Paco: next-themes |
| C9 | The browser theme bar (theme-color meta) uses an opaque color. It does not support transitions or transparent colors. Mix the overlay with the background to get the opaque color. | The bar color matches the page in each theme. Use one theme-color meta for each scheme, with a media attribute. Observed: paco.me (#1c1c1c dark, #ffffff light). | Emil: Drawer |

## Sources

Pages that I opened on 2026-10-30.

- Emil Kowalski: You Don't Need Animations
- Emil Kowalski: 7 Practical Animation Tips
- Emil Kowalski: Building a Toast Component
- Emil Kowalski: Building a Drawer Component
- Emil Kowalski: The Magic of Clip Path
- Emil Kowalski: Agents with Taste
- Emil Kowalski: Developing Taste
- Emil Kowalski: skill list
- Rasmus Andersson: Inter
- Benjamin De Cock: frontend-guidelines
- Paco Coursey: next-themes
- Paco Coursey: cmdk
- Shu Ding: react-wrap-balancer Live sites measured with computed CSS on 2026-10-30: emilkowal.ski, rsms.me, paco.me, shud.in.
==> skills/concept-ui/SKILL.md <==
---
name: concept-ui
description: Redesign the interface of an app or a screen from its conceptual model. First write the concepts and the object-action matrix, then map them to a layered, direct-manipulation, mobile-first interface, with checks that a reviewer can measure. For explicit use only, when the user invokes /concept-ui.
disable-model-invocation: true
---

# Concept UI

Design the model first and the screen second.
Do the three phases in sequence.
Do not start a phase before the previous phase is complete.
The target is the app or screen that the user names.
If the user names no target, ask for one.

## Phase 1: Conceptual model

Write `docs/concepts.md` (or the location that the project uses for design documents).
In this phase, do not write about layout, widgets, colours or screens.

1. Read the code and use the app.
   Make a list of the things that users see, make, change or delete.
2. For each concept (Jackson), write:
   - **Name**: one noun.
   - **Purpose**: one sentence.
     One concept has one purpose.
     If you find two purposes, make two concepts.
   - **State**: the data that the concept keeps.
   - **Actions**: the operations that change the state, as verbs.
   - **Operational principle**: one short scenario that shows how the concept fulfils its purpose ("after you do X, Y occurs").
3. For each object (ORCA), write:
   - **Objects**: the things that users manipulate.
   - **Relationships**: how each object connects to other objects, with cardinality.
   - **Calls-to-action**: the actions that a user can do on the object.
     For each action, write its frequency: many times a day, some times a day, or rarely.
   - **Attributes**: the data that a user sees or changes on the object.
4. Write how the concepts interact (synchronisations): when an action in one concept causes an action in another concept.
5. Use the same name for each concept and object in the code, the document and the interface.

## Phase 2: Object-action matrix

Make a table (Rosenberg).
Put the objects in the rows and the actions in the columns.
In each cell, write the interface location of that action on that object, or leave the cell empty.

Count the faults.
A fault is one of these:

- **Expected empty cell**: a user expects the action on the object, but the interface does not give it.
  Example: you can rename a project but not a file.
- **Displaced action**: the action is in the interface, but not on its object.
  Example: delete is in a global menu, not on the item.
- **Inconsistent action**: one action has different names, gestures or locations on different objects.

A cell that is empty because the action has no meaning for that object is not a fault.
Write "n/a" in it.
Record the fault count before the change.
Fix the faults in the model or the interface until the count is 0, or until the user accepts each remaining fault.

## Phase 3: Map the model to the interface

Each object in the model becomes a visible thing on the screen.
Each call-to-action becomes a control on, or next to, its object.
Then apply the rules below.
Each rule is a check.
Record pass or fail for each check, with the evidence.

1. **Posture and chrome budget** (Cooper).
   Decide if the app is sovereign (used for long periods, full screen) or transient (used for a short task, then closed).
   Check: write the posture in `docs/concepts.md`.
   For a transient app, chrome (bars, panels, menus) uses no more than 15% of the viewport height at 375 px width.
   For a sovereign app, the content area is the largest area on each screen.
2. **Content layer and floating control layer** (Liquid Glass).
   Content fills the screen.
   Controls and navigation float above the content in a separate layer.
   Check: content scrolls below the controls, and no control takes a fixed band of layout from the content.
3. **One z-position for each object** (Material 1).
   Define a small set of z-layers, for example: content 0, raised item 1, floating controls 2, sheet 3, dialog 4.
   Use shadow or blur to show height.
   Check: each object kind has one z-layer in a single table in the code (tokens or constants).
   No object changes layer, except during a lift or drag gesture.
4. **Direct manipulation** (Shneiderman).
   Users act on the visible object: drag, swipe, tap, edit in place.
   Each action shows its result at once.
   Each action is reversible.
   Check: no destructive action uses a confirm dialog.
   Each destructive action shows an undo control for at least 5 seconds, and the undo restores the previous state.
   The undo timer pauses while the page is hidden or while the user interacts with the undo control.
   Check: each pressable control changes on press (`:active` or `pointerdown`), not on release.
   It scales to 0.95–0.98 in 100–160 ms.
5. **A visible signifier for each action** (Norman).
   An affordance is what the user can do.
   A signifier shows the user that they can do it.
   Check: each cell of the matrix that is not empty has a visible signifier (icon, handle, label or hint).
   A gesture alone is not a signifier.
   A hover state alone is not a signifier, because touch screens have no hover.
6. **Fluid motion** (Apple, Designing Fluid Interfaces; Kowalski).
   Motion follows the finger, and the user can interrupt and redirect it at any time.
   Each animation has a purpose (M1).
   Use the action frequency from Phase 1.
   Check:
   - No animation for an action that users do many times a day, or that starts from the keyboard (M2).
     Press feedback (rule 4), and motion that a gesture drives and its release, are not part of this check.
   - Each other UI animation changes only `transform`, `opacity`, `clip-path` and a small `blur` (M12).
     It lasts 300 ms or less.
     Toasts, sheets, drawers and modals last 500 ms or less.
   - Elements that enter or exit use an ease-out curve, for example `cubic-bezier(0.23, 1, 0.32, 1)`.
     No UI animation uses `ease-in` (M4).
     For the sheet and drawer curve, see M5.
   - An element enters from the start scale in M7, with `opacity: 0`.
   - A popover scales from its trigger (M8).
     A modal scales from the centre.
   - During an animation, a new touch stops the animation and gives control to the finger.
     State that can change during an animation uses a CSS transition or a spring, not keyframes (M10).
   - A released drag continues with the finger velocity.
     It dismisses when it passes a distance threshold or a velocity threshold (K4).
     Thus a fast flick dismisses at any distance.
     Examples: a toast at 45 px or 0.11 px/ms, a drawer at 25% of its height or 0.4 px/ms.
   - Drag friction and pointer capture follow K5 (`setPointerCapture`).
   - With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, movement is replaced by a fade or no animation.
7. **Mobile first**.
   Check at 375 px width:
   - The page has no horizontal scroll.
   - Each tap target passes L8.
   - Content and controls respect the safe-area insets (`env(safe-area-inset-*)`), and the viewport meta tag has `viewport-fit=cover`.
   - The primary controls are in the bottom half of the screen, in thumb reach.
   - Each input has a font size of 16 px or more.
     The viewport meta tag has no `user-scalable=no` and no `maximum-scale`.
   - A full-height app shell uses `100dvh`, not `100vh`.
   - Each `:hover` rule is inside `@media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)`.
     Then check that the layout also works at desktop width (1280 px or more).
     Emulation does not show hover, safe-area, input-zoom or gesture faults.
     Record which checks need a real phone.
8. **Type and colour restraint** (Kowalski).
   Make the hierarchy with weight, colour and space first, and with size last.
   Check:
   - The body text column is 692 px wide or less (`max-width: 692px`) (T4).
   - Headings of 24 px or more have a letter-spacing from -0.04em to -0.02em.
   - Numbers that change in place use `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` (T2).
   - Content text uses no more than 3 colours: primary, secondary and one accent.
9. **Worst-case content** (Kowalski).
   Demo data makes a design look good.
   Real data breaks it (L10).
   Check: render each object with 0, 1 and many items, with missing optional fields, and with the longest value that the schema accepts.
   If the schema has no limit, use a long real name.
   No text overflows or clips.
   Counts use the correct plural ("1 member", with `Intl.PluralRules`).

When the target is a web page, audit it against `references/design-audit.md`.
Audit one page at a time.
Mark each rule Pass, Fail or N/A.
A Fail needs a fix or a written reason.

## Output

Give the user these items, in this sequence:

1. `docs/concepts.md`: concepts, ORCA objects, synchronisations and posture.
2. The object-action matrix before the change and after the change, each with its fault count.
3. The list of changes, each with the concept, object or rule that it serves.
4. The pass or fail result for each check in Phase 3, with the evidence.
5. Screenshots of each changed screen at 375 px and at 1280 px width, before and after the change.

If the project has a pull request flow, put items 2 to 5 in the pull request description.

## Worked example

The snowmobile redesign used this method: https://github.com/yujieteo/snowmobile/pull/3.
It wrote the conceptual model in `docs/concepts.md` first, decreased the matrix faults from 10 to 0, and then added a floating control layer, z-layers, direct manipulation with undo, signifiers and a mobile-first layout.

## Sources

| Work | What it gives |
| --- | --- |
| Daniel Jackson, *The Essence of Software* | Concepts, each with one purpose, state, actions and an operational principle |
| Daniel Rosenberg, *UX Magic* | Semantic interaction design: the conceptual model ("grammar") first, and the object-action matrix |
| Johnson & Henderson, *Conceptual Models* (2nd ed., 2024) | The model holds objects, attributes and operations, and no layout or widgets |
| Sophia Prater, OOUX / ORCA | Objects, relationships, calls-to-action, attributes |
| Material Design 1, Environment | A 3D space with light and shadow, and one z-position for each object |
| Apple, *Designing Fluid Interfaces* (WWDC18) | Motion that is responsive, interruptible and redirectable |
| Apple, Liquid Glass (WWDC25) | A control layer that floats above the content layer |
| Ben Shneiderman, direct manipulation | Visible objects, physical actions and rapid reversible actions |
| Don Norman, *The Design of Everyday Things* (revised ed.) | Affordances versus signifiers |
| Alan Cooper, application posture | Sovereign versus transient applications, which sets the chrome budget |
| Emil Kowalski, [emilkowalski/skills](https://github.com/emilkowalski/skills) (MIT), Sonner, Vaul, [emilkowal.ski](https://emilkowal.ski/), [aiforui.dev](https://aiforui.dev/) | Motion by action frequency, easing and duration limits, press feedback, mobile web fixes, type restraint and worst-case data |
==> skills/guardrails/SKILL.md <==
---
name: guardrails
description: Find the parts that a project has (a fixed bug, comments, types, states, hot paths, web pages, a simulation, tuned parameters, pure functions, parsers, printed output, a deterministic transform, dead code, C code, an app) and add the related lint rule, type, test, budget check, state model, replay, property test, fuzz target, expect test, output replay, dead-code check, sanitizer build or verification rule in the tools of that project. For explicit use only, when the user invokes /guardrails.
disable-model-invocation: true
---

# Guardrails

Make the tools stop a fault, so that no person must remember a rule.
The target is the project or the bug that the user names.
If the user names no target, ask for one.
Do the steps in sequence.
Each step starts with "Applies when".
If the project does not have that part, do not change the project for that step.
Use the language, linter, test runner and build of the project.
Add a new tool only when the project has none for that job.
Before you keep a new check that runs (for example a lint rule, a type, a test, a budget, a model, a dead-code build failure, a sanitizer build or a fuzz target), break the code that it guards.
See the check fail, then remove the break.
A rule in the agent instructions has no break.
A raised budget or a new excluded file needs the approval of the owner and a reason in the change.
Never disable a check: fix the code, or delete the check with the approval of the owner.

## Step 0: Find the parts

Read the build files, the linter config, the tests and the main loop.
Write one table:

| Part | Found | Evidence (`file:line`) | Step |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |

Give one row to each deterministic transform: a part whose output depends only on its input (for example a compiler, a code generator, a renderer, a serializer, a build step or app logic with an output that a test can capture).
Write "no" for each part that the project does not have.
Do not add a part to make a step apply.

## Step 1: A bug becomes a rule

Applies when: the user names a bug, or a step below finds one.

1. Write a lint rule that catches the pattern of the bug.
   When no lint rule can catch it, write a failing test.
2. Run it on the code before the fix and see it fail for the right reason.
   Then run it on the fix and see it pass.
3. Name the bug in the rule name or the test name.

Reason: a fixed bug comes back when the next change does not know about it.
A rule or a test stops it on each run.

## Step 2: No comments

Applies when: the project has source code.
Tool directives (for example a lint disable, a shebang or a type pragma) and licence headers are not comments for this rule, but item 7 applies to a lint disable.

1. Choose the excluded files.
   Exclude a file that a tool writes (generated code, a lock file, build output) or that comes from another project (vendored or copied code).
   Start from the paths that the ignore files, the package manager and the build already name.
   Add a copied file only when its source and licence are known.
   Write the list once, in the lint config.
2. Add a lint rule that bans comments in all other files.
   Keep only a comment that links to an outside source: an issue, an RFC, a paper or a credit to an author.
   Find comments with a parser or a lexer of the language: the comment rule of the project linter, a tree-sitter query, or the tokenizer of the language.
3. Do not find comments with a text pattern (for example grep for `#` or `//`).
   A text pattern also matches a colour value such as `#fff`, a URL and a string.
4. Change each comment that the rule finds.
   When a comment explains code, give the code a better name.
   When a comment states a behavior, write a test with that behavior as its name.
5. When a fact fits no name and no test (for example a build tool version or a hash file), put it in code that runs.
   Use a named constant, a type, an assertion in the build script, or an error message that prints when the fact is broken.
6. Never move a fact into a README or another prose file.
   Delete a comment only when its fact is in code that runs, from before or from item 4 or 5.
7. Do not keep a lint disable of a rule that catches real bugs (for example a type error or an unused result).
   Fix the code, then remove the disable.

Reason: a comment does not run, so it becomes wrong when the code changes, and a README becomes wrong in the same way.
A name, a test or a check stays true or fails.
A parser reads comments as the compiler reads them, so it does not match strings or colour values.

## Step 3: Types

Applies when: the project has a static type checker (for example TypeScript, Rust, OCaml, or Python with mypy), and a value can hold an illegal state.

1. Turn on the strictest mode of the type checker that the build can pass.
   Make the build fail on a type error.
2. Find each value that can hold an illegal state: flags that exclude each other, optional fields that are always set together, and plain strings or numbers for ids and units.
3. Change each one to a type that cannot hold the illegal state: a tagged union for exclusive states, one record for fields that are set together, and a distinct type for each id and unit.
4. Parse outside data (JSON, a config file, environment variables or a request) into a type at the boundary.
   Inside the boundary, use only the parsed type.
5. Derive each type from the schema that owns the shape (for example a database schema, an OpenAPI file or a protobuf file).
   Do not write the same shape again by hand.
6. Each illegal state that the code can reach is a bug: it goes to step 1.

Reason: the type checker gives feedback on each build, and it checks a rule in all of the code at once.
A test checks only the cases that it runs.

## Step 4: States

Applies when: a major system keeps a state that changes on events (for example a session, an upload, a connection or a turn).

1. Run `/state-machine` on each major system.
   Make one machine file for each system.
2. Each bug that it proves goes to step 1.
3. When an invariant is critical (for example money, data loss or access control) and the state table is too large to check by hand, use a model checker.
4. When pstack is installed, run `/create-formal-verification-skill` for that invariant.
   Else, write the model (for example in TLA+) from the machine file.
5. In the model checker config or a check script, record the files that the model covers and a hash of these files.
   Make the project test or build check fail when a covered file changes after the record.
   To pass, run the model checker again and update the hash.
   Do not use a comment or a prose note for the record.

Reason: state bugs live in combinations of flags that no test tries.
The state table finds them, and a model checker tries all combinations.
Formal methods were expensive, but an agent makes a model cheap to write.

## Step 5: Hot paths

Applies when: code runs each frame, each tick or each sample (for example a render loop, a game loop or an audio callback).

1. Put `.hot` in the name of each file with such code, before the extension (for example `physics.hot.ts`).
   Move cold code out of these files.
2. Add a lint rule that applies only to `*.hot.*` files and bans allocation: object literals, array literals, closures and `new`.
3. Change each allocation that the rule finds: reuse a buffer, a pool or a value from the setup code.

Reason: an allocation in a loop that runs each frame causes garbage collection pauses.
The file name tells the reader and the linter which code is hot.

## Step 6: Web page budget

Applies when: the project ships a web page.

1. List each route or page template (for example the home page, the article template and the tool template).
   Do not list each article.
2. Add a build step that finds each page in the build output itself.
   The step gives each page the budget of its route or template.
   A page that the list does not name gets the default budget.
   The step never skips a page.
3. For each page, the step measures the gzipped size of each file that the first load of that page downloads.
   Count the shared files in the total of each page.
   The step fails when the total of a page is larger than its budget.
4. Use the budget of the owner for each route or template and for the default.
   When the owner gives no budget, use the largest current total of the pages of that route or template, rounded up to the next 10 KB.
   For the default, use the largest current total of all pages, rounded up in the same way.
   Report the budget of each route or template and of the default.

Reason: a user can arrive on any page from a link, and the first download sets the load time on a slow network.
Each change adds a few kilobytes, and no review sees the total.

## Step 7: Deterministic simulation

Applies when: the project has a simulation (code that changes its state from inputs over many steps, for example a game, a physics model or a scheduler).

1. Make each step a function of the state and the inputs only.
   Use a fixed time step and a seeded random number generator.
2. Add a lint rule that bans the wall clock and unseeded random numbers in simulation files.
3. Record the seed and the inputs of a run.
   Add a test that replays a recorded run and compares a hash of the final state.
4. Add a test that runs the simulation with many seeds and checks the invariants of the state after each step.
   Record each seed that fails as a run for item 3, and give the bug to step 1.
5. When a server accepts a result of a run (for example a score), make the server replay the inputs and compare the result.

Reason: a run that does not replay cannot be debugged from a report.
A posted result that the server does not replay can be false.

## Step 8: Tuned parameters

Applies when: a parameter has a target value that a run can measure (for example a survival rate for each difficulty).
Step 7 must apply first.

1. Write a headless bot that runs the simulation without the interface.
2. Add a test that runs the bot many times for each parameter set and fails when the measured value is outside the target range.
3. Report each parameter set that fails, with its measured value.
   Do not change the parameter without the owner.

Reason: a person tunes by feel and by a small number of runs.
A bot measures the target on each change.

## Step 9: Property tests and fuzz targets

Applies when: the project has a pure function with a rule that holds for all inputs (for example decode after encode gives the input), or a parser of outside input.

1. Use the property test library of the language (for example fast-check, Hypothesis, proptest or QuickCheck).
   Write one property test for each rule.
2. For each parser of outside input, add a fuzz target for the fuzzer of the language (for example cargo-fuzz, Go fuzzing or Atheris).
   Make the target fail on a crash or a hang.
3. Keep each input that fails in the library or the fuzzer as a fixed test case.
   Give the bug to step 1.

Reason: a person writes the test inputs that the person expects.
A generator tries inputs that nobody expects, and it makes each failure small.

## Step 10: Expect tests

Applies when: code makes output that a person reads to check it (for example compiler or parser output, a report, a rendered table or a state trace), or a test asserts many fields one by one.

1. Use the snapshot or expect test tool of the test runner (for example Vitest snapshots, insta, ppx_expect or syrupy).
2. Write a test that prints the output as readable text and keeps the printed text in the test or in a fixture file next to it.
   Print only what a user of the code can see, not internal state.
3. Make the test fail on each difference and show the diff.
   Update the kept text only with a command, so that the change shows in the review.

Reason: the printed text shows the behavior in one place, as the fixture tests of a compiler do.
The reviewer reads one diff of text, not each assertion.

## Step 11: Output replay

Applies when: step 0 found a deterministic transform.
Use step 10 for a small output that a person reads.
Use this step for a large generated output (for example a whole C file or a GPU kernel).
Step 7 stays for a simulation.
Source of the idea: https://github.com/tinygrad/tinygrad/blob/master/test/external/process_replay/README.md

1. Add a capture switch (for example an environment variable).
   When the existing tests run with it, the transform writes each input to a local cache outside the repository.
2. Add a replay command.
   It runs the base commit and the branch, each in an isolated copy (for example a git worktree).
   It gives each captured input to the transform of each commit and diffs the outputs.
3. Treat each commit as a refactor: the command fails on each diff.
   Nothing in the commit (for example a title tag or a trailer) can turn the check off.
4. When a change must change the output, the command prints the diff.
   It passes only when the person who runs it accepts that diff on that run (for example with an `--accept` flag).
5. When more than 20% of the outputs differ, the command stops early and reports a large change.
   Without an accept, this is a failure.
6. Run the command locally before a review.
   Do not add it to CI.

Reason: a refactor of a transform can change one output in a thousand, and no test asserts that output.
The inputs of the existing tests find it.
An accept on each run makes a person read the diff, and a commit cannot skip the check.
The early stop saves time when the diff is too large to read.

## Step 12: Dead code

Applies when: the project has source code.

1. Use the dead-code rule of the project linter, or add a dead-code tool of the language (for example knip, cargo-udeps, or the unused warnings of the compiler as errors).
   Make the build fail on each unreferenced file, export, function or dependency.
2. Use the excluded files of step 2.
   Give each public entry point to the tool as a root.
3. Delete each unreferenced symbol that the tool finds.
   Ask the owner before you delete a public API.
4. Write this rule in the agent instructions of the project: before you end a change, find the references of each symbol that the change replaced.
   Use the language server or the compiler.
   At zero references, delete the symbol.

Reason: an agent adds new code next to old code, because a deletion needs proof.
The reference query gives the proof, and the build fails until the old code goes.

## Step 13: C sanitizers

Applies when: the project ships C or C++ code, also C that a compiler generates (for example from Goose).

1. Add a test build that compiles with `-fsanitize=undefined,address` and `-fno-sanitize-recover=all`.
2. Run all tests on that build in CI.
   A sanitizer report fails the test.
3. Each fault that a sanitizer reports goes to step 1.
   For generated C, fix the generator, not the generated file.

Reason: undefined behavior and memory faults often give the correct result in a test, then fail on another compiler or input.
A sanitizer stops the test at the fault.

## Step 14: Verify

Applies when: the project has an app that a person or a program starts (for example a web site, a server, a command or a game).

1. Find the verification skill or command of the project (for example a `verify-*` skill).
   It starts the app and proves a change on the path of a user.
2. When the project has one, write this rule in the agent instructions of the project: before you say that a change is done, run it and show its output.
3. When the project has none, do not write one.
   Name `/create-verification-skill` in Done as an open question.

Reason: tests show that the parts work, but only a start of the app shows that a change works for a user.
The agent then gives proof, not a guess.

## Done

- The part table of step 0.
- For each step that applies: the rule, type, test or check, its path and its first run output.
- For each check that runs (for example a lint rule, a type, a test, a budget, a model, a dead-code build failure, a sanitizer build or a fuzz target): the break that it caught.
- For step 6: for each route or template and for the default, the largest current page total and the budget.
- For each step that does not apply: one line that names the missing part.
- The open questions for the owner: the budget of each route or template and of the default, the target values, the excluded files of step 2, the bugs that the new rules found, and `/create-verification-skill` when step 14 found no verification skill.
==> skills/README.md <==
# skills.md

Agent skills for Claude Code that you invoke by name.

## Skills

- [concept-ui](concept-ui/SKILL.md): UI redesign.
  Redesign an app or a screen from its conceptual model, then map the model to a layered, direct-manipulation, mobile-first interface.
- [guardrails](guardrails/SKILL.md): project rules.
  Find the parts that a project has, then add the lint rule, type, test, page budget, state model, replay, property test, fuzz target, expect test, output replay, dead-code check, sanitizer build or verification rule that stops the related fault, in the tools of that project.
- [schematic-emil](schematic-emil/SKILL.md): figures.
  Draw a scientific schematic or a patent drawing sheet in the look of [emilkowal.ski](https://emilkowal.ski), for the web, papers, slides and patents.
  It is a style for the [schematic-figure](https://github.com/lgwrightlab/schematic-figure) and patent-drawing skills by Jinchen Zhao and Logan Wright (LGW Lab, Yale University).
  Read their [blog post](https://lgwrightlab.github.io/schematic-figure/).
  Install those two skills from commit `09d8b6e`.
- [state-machine](state-machine/SKILL.md): requirement and code review.
  Model code or a requirement as one state table, delete the rows and requirements that are not necessary, show a simpler design, prove each bug with a failing test, and review it on an HTML board.
  The CLI is `state-machine/bin/sm-axi.ts` (Bun, no dependencies).
  Example: [femahoy chat](state-machine/examples/femahoy-chat/).

## Install for Claude Code

Copy or symlink the skill folder into `~/.claude/skills/`:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/yujieteo/skills.md
ln -s "$PWD/skills.md/concept-ui" ~/.claude/skills/concept-ui
ln -s "$PWD/skills.md/guardrails" ~/.claude/skills/guardrails
ln -s "$PWD/skills.md/schematic-emil" ~/.claude/skills/schematic-emil
ln -s "$PWD/skills.md/state-machine" ~/.claude/skills/state-machine
ln -s "$PWD/skills.md/state-machine/bin/sm-axi.ts" ~/.local/bin/sm-axi
```

`schematic-emil` needs the upstream skills at the pinned commit:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/lgwrightlab/schematic-figure
git -C schematic-figure checkout 09d8b6e98d04b91de6e8b6632b3356b9fc237417
ln -s "$PWD/schematic-figure/.claude/skills/schematic-figure" ~/.claude/skills/schematic-figure
ln -s "$PWD/schematic-figure/.claude/skills/patent-drawing" ~/.claude/skills/patent-drawing
```

To install for one project only, use `.claude/skills/` in that project.

## Invoke

`concept-ui`, `guardrails` and `state-machine` set `disable-model-invocation: true`, so the agent does not start them automatically.
An agent can start `schematic-emil` when the user names it in a task.
Type the skill name and the target:

```text
/concept-ui the settings screen
/guardrails the game in ~/src/game
/schematic-emil a figure of the cache flow for the blog post
/state-machine the upload flow in src/upload.ts
```

## Develop

The tools run on [Bun](https://bun.sh) with strict TypeScript.

```sh
bun install
bun run check
```

`bun run check` runs `tsc --noEmit`, the lint of `scripts/lint.ts` (no comment without a link, no `any`, no type assertion, no non-null assertion, no `JSON.parse` result that is not `unknown`), `knip` (dead code) and `bun test`.

To update an expected output, run `bun test -u` and read the diff.

## Process replay

A change to a transform (`sm-axi check`, `sm-axi render`, `useEmil`, `emilTheme`, `patentFigure`) must not change its outputs, unless you accept the difference.

```sh
bun run capture
bun run replay
bun run replay --accept
```

`capture` runs the tests and keeps each input of a transform in `~/.cache/skills-md-replay`.
`replay` runs each input on the merge base with `origin/main` and on `HEAD` (each in a copy of the commit), and fails on each difference.
`--accept` accepts the differences on that run.
`--base <commit>` changes the base.
When more than 20% of the inputs differ, `replay` stops and reports a large change.
Commit before you run `replay`.
Do not add `replay` to CI.
==> skills/schematic-emil/SKILL.md <==
---
name: schematic-emil
description: Draw a scientific schematic figure or a patent drawing sheet in the look of Emil Kowalski (emilkowal.ski) for a blog post, a paper, a slide or a patent. It runs the upstream schematic-figure or patent-drawing skill with this style. Use when the user asks for schematic-emil by name.
---

# Schematic figure in the Emil Kowalski style

This skill is a style for two upstream skills.
It does not copy or change them.

- Upstream: [lgwrightlab/schematic-figure](https://github.com/lgwrightlab/schematic-figure) by Jinchen Zhao and Logan Wright (LGW Lab, Yale University), MIT licence.
  Blog post: [Schematic figure](https://lgwrightlab.github.io/schematic-figure/).
- Pinned commit: `09d8b6e98d04b91de6e8b6632b3356b9fc237417` (2026-10-14).
  Install its `schematic-figure` and `patent-drawing` skills unchanged.
- Style values: copied from the CSS that [emilkowal.ski](https://emilkowal.ski) serves, and from Radix Colors 3.0.0 (the family of the site gray).
- Font: Inter 4.1 (`fonts/InterVariable.woff2`), SIL Open Font License 1.1 (`fonts/LICENSE.txt`).

## Invoke

```text
/schematic-emil <request> [venue: web | journal | slide | poster | patent] [animate]
```

If the request names no venue, infer it: a blog post is `web`, a paper is `journal`, a talk is `slide`, a patent is `patent`.

## Process

1. Run the upstream `schematic-figure` skill with `style=schematic-emil`.
   For a patent sheet, run the upstream `patent-drawing` skill.
   Keep all upstream steps, options and defaults (also depth `full`).
2. Build `style.ts` from this folder into `figure/emil.js` with `bun build <skill-folder>/style.ts --outfile figure/emil.js`.
   Copy `fonts/` to `figure/fonts/`.
   Run the upstream tools with `--root <project>`.
3. In `figure/figure.js`, call `E.useEmil(F, L, venue)` first in `draw()`.
   It sets the font, the gray and colour tokens, the text sizes and the line weights for the venue.
   It returns `{s, r, line, text}`: the scale, the corner radii in pt, and the sizes.
4. Draw with the tokens in `tokens.ts` (`style.ts` exports them).
   Do not write a hex value that is not a token.
5. Run the upstream gate, `node tools/check.mjs --root <project> --fig figure/figure.js`.
   A FAIL stops delivery.

## Style rules

- **Font.**
  Inter for all text.
  `useEmil` loads it and sets the site font features `"cv01", "ss03"`.
  Use weight 500 (`E.weight.name`) for the name of a part, 400 for other text, and 600 for a title.
- **Gray first.**
  Draw most marks in the 12-step gray (`E.gray[100]` to `E.gray[1200]`).
  Background 100, text 1200, secondary text 1100, lines and outlines 700 to 900.
- **Colour.**
  Add a colour only where a part must look different from the other parts.
  Use as many hues as the figure needs, from `E.hue` (blue, sky, grass, amber, orange, tomato, violet).
  Step 3 is an area tint, step 7 an outline, step 9 a solid mark, step 11 a line or a label.
  Text on a step 9 solid mark uses `E.onSolid[hue]` (white on blue, grass, orange, tomato and violet, gray 1200 light on sky and amber).
  It has the same value in light and dark mode, as step 9 does.
- **Cards.**
  A box is a card: fill gray 100, a 1 px ring in gray 400, and 12 px corners (`E.card(r.card)`).
  A small tag has 6 px corners (`r.chip`).
  Fill a card with a step 3 tint and outline it with step 7 only when it must stand out.
- **Lines.**
  Borders are 1 px (`line.thin`).
  A main stroke is 2 px (`line.bold`).
  Use no shadows, gradients or glows.
  An upstream part that has a gradient (for example `L.laser` or `L.camera`) does not have the site look: draw a card in its place.
- **Text.**
  Labels are 14 px and secondary text is 12 px.
  The site makes a heading with weight, not with size.
  Do not make a label larger to show that it is important.
- **Space.**
  Keep the site's open layout: 16 px padding in a card and 24 px between groups.
- **Venue rules win.**
  The size, minimum text size, line weight and resolution come from the upstream format table (`reference/style.md`).
  `useEmil` scales the site px to each venue, so that the 14 px label is 7 pt in a journal, 18 pt on a slide and 28 pt on a poster.
  A web figure uses the journal scale: draw it 120 mm wide and show it at 100% of a 640-700 px column.

## Web output

- Call `E.emilTheme(F)` last in `draw()`.
  Each colour becomes `var(--color-<token>, <light value>)`.
  The gray names are the names that the site uses (`--color-gray-1200`).
- **Dark mode.**
  Put the SVG inline in the page.
  It then takes the colour tokens of the page, and it switches under a `.dark` class.
  A standalone SVG switches with `prefers-color-scheme`.
  A standalone SVG in an `<img>` cannot load Inter, so it shows the system font.
- **Static by default.**
  The option `animate` calls `E.emilTheme(F, { animate: true })`.
  The panels then come in one after the other with opacity and a 4 px move, in 300 ms with a 60 ms stagger, with the `emil-design-eng` ease-out `cubic-bezier(0.23, 1, 0.32, 1)`.
  Read the installed `emil-design-eng` skill before you add or change a motion.
  No motion occurs under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`.
  The PNG and the PDF show the end state.
- Papers, slides and patents stay light.

## Patent sheets

Make two versions of each sheet from one figure file.
Use a query on the figure path to select the version:

```js
import * as E from './emil.js';
const COLOUR = /[?&]v=colour/.test(import.meta.url);
export const figure = E.patentFigure({ sheet: 'letter', sheetId: '1/1', name: 'fig1' }, COLOUR);
export default function draw(F, L) {
  E.useEmil(F, L, COLOUR ? 'patent-colour' : 'patent');
  // draw into F.drawing, as patent-drawing step 4 says
}
```

- **Version 1, colour** (`--fig "figure/figure.js?v=colour"`): this style at the patent sizes, for the user's own use.
  Give each part a step 3 fill.
  The gate checks it with the slide rules (text 14 pt or more).
- **Version 2, filing** (`--fig figure/figure.js`): black and white with no gray fill, under 37 CFR 1.84.
  The upstream patent profile and the patent gate apply.
  Only the font changes to Inter.
- Reason: 37 CFR 1.84 accepts colour only after a petition, and the EPO and the PCT do not accept colour.

## Generated images

All vector marks and labels stay in code.
When a figure needs a raster image (for example an illustrated hero part or a texture), ask Codex with GPT-6.1-Sol to make it:

```sh
codex exec -m gpt-6.1-sol "<prompt>"
```

Put the style rules in the prompt: the gray background, the hues from `E.hue`, flat shapes with no shadow.
Place the image with `L.image` and label it in vector.
