==> skills/state-machine/examples/femahoy-chat/README.md <==
# Example: the femahoy chat

The first real run of the skill, on femahoy at `321483d6` (read only: no change to femahoy).

| File | Content |
| --- | --- |
| [chat.sm.md](chat.sm.md) | The machines `chat` (the agent process in the daemon) and `job` (the page, from request to mesh, solve and report), the proposal, the requirements and the bug |
| [check.toon](check.toon) | `sm-axi check chat.sm.md` |
| [chat.html](chat.html) | `sm-axi render chat.sm.md`: the review page |
| [tests/](tests/) | Failing tests. The `TARGET` constant of each file names the femahoy path that it targets and shows in the failure message |

## Results

- B1, a bug: after Stop, entries of the stopped agent still show.
  `tests/chat_stale_line.rs` fails on `321483d6`: the chat shows `end done in 0.0 s` and `note Terminated: 15` after `note stopped the agent`.
  The `end` entry hides the Rerun button (`daemon/page.html:819`).
  The cause: `Chat::follow` pushes each entry of each agent (`daemon/chat.rs:386`), and the stderr thread does the same (`daemon/chat.rs:313`).
- The hidden state `broken`: a message that cannot reach the agent drops the input but keeps the chat `running` (`daemon/chat.rs:264`).
  `tests/chat_write_failure.rs` fails on `321483d6` with `state=running` after the write failure.
  It pins the proposal, not a bug: the owner decides.
- `job` has no state in the code: the page only shows entries.
  `asked` and `meshed` are equivalent, and `reported` is a pass-through.
  R8 ("at least 2 meshes") has a row (`solved | open`), but no guard stops a report after 1 mesh: only the instructions of the agent ask for it.
- 6 rows have no requirement.
  The owner decides on each one on the board.

## Refactor plan (not done: a separate approved step)

The proposal `chat` has 16 rows, not 20, keeps each requirement, and passes the 7 checks.

1. Replace the flags `reading`, `turn`, `sent`, `taken`, `input` and `agent` of `Log` (`daemon/chat.rs:70`) with one field: `enum Agent { None, Resting { child, input }, Working { child, input, started_ms, unread } }`.
   `running()` becomes a match.
2. A write failure in `ask` (`daemon/chat.rs:264`) ends the agent (`Log::end`) and goes to `None`.
   The state `broken` goes.
3. `follow` and the stderr thread push an entry only when `log.current(pid)` (`daemon/chat.rs:386` and `daemon/chat.rs:313`).
   This also fixes B1.
4. Keep `tests/chat_stop.rs` green.
   Make `tests/chat_stale_line.rs` and `tests/chat_write_failure.rs` green.

Run the tests in a femahoy checkout:

```sh
cp state-machine/examples/femahoy-chat/tests/*.rs <femahoy>/crates/fh/tests/
cargo test -p fh --test chat_stale_line --test chat_write_failure
```
==> skills/state-machine/references/format.md <==
# The machine file (`.sm.md`)

One Markdown file holds the requirements, the bugs and each machine.
`sm-axi` reads only the parts below.
Other text is free.

```markdown
# Upload: from pick to stored            <- title of the review page

Source: app at 1a2b3c, read only.        <- intro text, shown under the title

## Requirements

- R1: A person can cancel an upload at any time. — DESIGN.md:12
- R2: A failed upload can be tried again. — issue #40

## Bugs

- B1: Cancel during retry leaves the spinner on. — tests/upload_cancel.rs fails on 1a2b3c

## Machine upload

initial: idle
final: stored

| state | event | guard | next | req | source |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| idle | pick | | sending | R1 | src/upload.ts:20 |
| sending | done | ok | stored | R1 | src/upload.ts:41 |
| sending | done | else | failed | R2 | src/upload.ts:44 |
| failed | retry | | sending | R2 | src/upload.ts:60 |
| * | cancel | | idle | R1 | src/upload.ts:70 |

## Proposal upload

(the same form: a simpler design of the machine `upload`)
```

## Sections

| Section | Content |
| --- | --- |
| `# <title>` | The page title. The lines after it, up to the first machine, are the intro. |
| `## Requirements` | `- <id>: <text> — <source>`. The source is required (` -- ` also works). |
| `## Bugs` | `- <id>: <text> — <failing test>`. Only bugs with a failing test. |
| `## Machine <name>` | `initial:`, `final:` (a comma list, can be empty) and the table. |
| `## Proposal <name>` | A simpler design of the machine `<name>`, in the same form. |

## Columns

| Column | Rule |
| --- | --- |
| `state` | A state name, or `*` for each non-final state. |
| `event` | The event name. Use the same word for the same event in each row. |
| `guard` | Empty, a condition, or `else`. Write `!x` or `not x` for the opposite of `x`. Write `or` and `and`, not `\|\|`. |
| `next` | The next state, the same state (a loop with an effect), or `-` (the event is ignored: no effect). |
| `req` | Requirement ids, separated by spaces or commas. Empty or `-` is no requirement. |
| `source` | `file:line` of the code, or the requirement id. |

A `*` row applies to each non-final state that has no own row for its event.
A `*` row with a guard applies to each non-final state that has no own row for its event and that guard.

## Findings

`sm-axi check` prints `machines`, `bugs`, `simplify`, `checks` and `proposals`.
Each finding has `kind`, `machine`, `at` (`state`, `state/event` or an id), `line` (`L<n>` of the file) and `detail`.

| Kind | Group | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `unreachable` | check | No path from `initial`. |
| `dead-end` | check | Not final, and no row goes to another state. |
| `unhandled` | check | A reachable non-final state has no row for these events. |
| `two-transitions` | check | 2 or more rows on one state and event, and the guards overlap (the same guard, or one row has no guard). |
| `guard-gap` | check | Guarded rows with no `else`, and not exactly `x` and `!x`. |
| `row-no-req` | check | A row with no requirement, or with an id that is not in `## Requirements`. |
| `req-no-row` | check | A requirement that no row of a machine cites. |
| `merge-states` | simplify | Equivalent states (minimisation): the same events go to equivalent states, and the same events are ignored. |
| `pass-through` | simplify | One row in, one row out, no guard, and no requirement of its own. |
| `global-event` | simplify | One event, guard and next state in 3 or more states. |
| `flag-guard` | simplify | One guard term in 3 or more rows of 2 or more states. |
| `flag-states` | simplify | 3 or more state names made only of name parts that other states share (`on_open`, `off_open`). |
| `function` | simplify | 2 states or fewer, or no branch and no loop. |

`rows_per_req` is the rows of a machine divided by the requirements that it cites.

A proposal has `status` `shown` or `hidden`, with the reason.
It shows only if it cites each requirement that the machine cites, has fewer rows, and has no check finding.

Exit codes: 0 no check finding, 1 one or more check findings, 2 a usage or parse error.
==> skills/state-machine/references/review.md <==
# The review page

`sm-axi render <file>` writes one HTML file with no network files.
It shows, from the most important item:

1. The verdict: proven bugs, check findings, simplifications, rows per requirement.
2. The proven bugs with their failing tests.
3. The findings in the order of the work: question the requirements, simplify, kill bugs.
   Each finding links to its row.
4. For each machine: the diagram and the simulator.
   A shown proposal is side by side with the current machine.
5. The rows, with Approve, Reject and a comment on each row.
   Then the requirements, with the same controls.

## The board

- With lavish-axi: `lavish-axi <file>.html`.
  "Queue the review" queues one tracked batch with each decided id (`<machine>-L<line>` for a row, the id for a requirement).
  Then the owner sends it.
  Read it with `lavish-axi poll <file>.html`.
- Without lavish-axi: open the file in a browser.
  "Copy the review" puts the decisions on the clipboard as text, one id on each line.
- A rejected row is deleted with its requirement.
  A comment needs an answer in the next version.
- After the changes, run `check` and `render` again, and reply on the board with `lavish-axi reply <file>.html --agent-reply "<what changed>"`.

## Look

The tokens of the owner's site (`https://teoyujie.org/shared/kowalski.css`), light only, as the site: the gray scale, the Inter and IBM Plex Mono stacks, the strong ease-out curve, the `.button` look, 44 px tap areas, and hover only under `(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)`.

## Motion

Names from animation-vocabulary.
Each motion has a purpose.

| Motion | Name | Values | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| The current-state marker moves along the transition | Translate, a Tween along the edge path (WAAPI) | `transform` only, 200 ms, `cubic-bezier(0.23, 1, 0.32, 1)` | Spatial consistency: the reviewer sees which edge the event took |
| A button press | Press / Tap feedback | `scale(0.97)`, 150 ms ease-out | Feedback |
| A fired row, the current node | none (instant colour) | | A colour change at the arrival does not need motion |

- A keyboard-fired event (`click` with `detail` 0) moves the marker at once: never animate a keyboard action.
- Reduced motion: the marker moves at once, and the press scale is 1.
- A second event during a move finishes the first move, then starts the next one.

## review-animations verdict of the template

| Before | After | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Marker jumps to the next state | WAAPI `transform` along the edge, 200 ms strong ease-out | Spatial consistency; under 300 ms; ease-out answers the click at once |
| Every fire animates | No movement for a keyboard fire or reduced motion | Keyboard actions get no animation; reduced motion is honoured |
| Node colour fades | Instant colour at the arrival | No second motion that competes with the marker |

Approve: `transform` only, no `transition: all`, no `ease-in`, hover gated, reduced motion honoured.
==> skills/state-machine/SKILL.md <==
---
name: state-machine
description: Model the states of code or of a written requirement as one table, question each requirement, delete what is not necessary, show a simpler design, prove each bug with a failing test, and give the owner an HTML review board with a step-through simulator. For explicit use only, when the user invokes /state-machine.
disable-model-invocation: true
---

# State machine

Make a state machine smaller before you make it correct.
Do the steps in sequence.
Never fix code before the owner approves the row on the board.
The target is the code, the requirement, or both, that the user names.
If the user names no target, ask for one.

The CLI is `bin/sm-axi.ts` in this skill folder (Bun, no dependencies).
Run it as `bun <skill-folder>/bin/sm-axi.ts`, or link it as `sm-axi` on the PATH.
It prints compact TOON.
The `help` lines of each output give the next command.

## Step 0: Write the machine file

Read [references/format.md](references/format.md) before you write the first file.

1. Write `<name>.sm.md` next to the design documents of the project, or in a scratch folder when the project is read-only.
2. Write the requirements first.
   Each requirement has an id, its text, and its source: a rule in a design document (`DESIGN.md:36`), an issue, or the exact words of the owner.
   Do not write a requirement that has no source.
3. Write one row for each transition: `state | event | guard | next | req | source`.
   - From code: the source is `file:line` of the code that does the transition.
   - From a requirement only: the source is the requirement id.
4. When the code keeps its state in flags (booleans, counters, `Option` fields), name the real states and write each flag test as a guard.
   Write a state that the flags allow but no name covers (for example, a closed input while the turn runs).
   These hidden states are where bugs live.
5. Keep machines flat.
   A parallel region is a second `## Machine` in the same file.

## Step 1: Question the requirements

Run `sm-axi check <file>`.
Read the `row-no-req` and `req-no-row` findings.

- A row with no requirement: the code does it, but nobody asked for it.
  Ask the owner on the board: approve it (then name the requirement and its source), or reject it (then it is deleted).
- A requirement with no row: nothing does it.
  Ask whether it is still necessary.
- Do not invent a requirement to remove a finding.
  The finding is the question.
- For each requirement, ask: did a real run show the need?
  A requirement added "in case" is a candidate to delete.

## Step 2: Delete

After the review, delete each rejected row and its requirement from the file.
Delete each requirement that the owner rejected, and each row that only it cited.
Run `sm-axi check` again.

## Step 3: Simplify

Read the `simplify` findings of `sm-axi check`:

| Finding | Question for the design |
| --- | --- |
| `merge-states` | The states do the same thing. Can they be one state? |
| `pass-through` | One way in, one way out, no guard, no own requirement. Delete the state. |
| `global-event` | Many states do the same on one event. Write one `*` row. |
| `flag-guard` | One flag in many guards. Is it a hidden state, or a second machine? |
| `flag-states` | The state names combine flags. Make one machine for each flag. |
| `function` | No branch and no loop. A plain function is better than a machine. |

Also look at `rows_per_req`.
A high ratio means that the machine is larger than its requirements.

When a simpler design exists, write it as `## Proposal <machine>` in the same file.
`sm-axi` shows a proposal only if it keeps each accepted requirement, has fewer rows and passes the 7 checks.
Else `check` says why it is hidden.
Do not argue for a hidden proposal.

## Step 4: Kill bugs

The 7 checks run on what remains:

1. `unreachable`: no path from the initial state.
2. `dead-end`: no exit and not final.
3. `unhandled`: an event of the machine with no row in a state.
   Write the row, or write it as ignored (`next` is `-`).
4. `two-transitions`: two rows on one state and event with overlapping guards.
5. `guard-gap`: the guards of one state and event do not cover all cases.
   Add an `else` row.
6. `row-no-req`: a row with no requirement (step 1).
7. `req-no-row`: a requirement with no row (step 1).

For each finding that is a fault in the code (not only in the table):

1. Write a failing test in the test style of the target project.
   Name the row of the machine in the test name.
2. Run it and see it fail for the right reason.
   When the project is read-only, run it in a scratch copy.
3. Add the bug to `## Bugs` with the test path as its proof.
4. Do not fix it.
   The owner decides on the board.

## Step 5: Review

Run `sm-axi render <file>`.
It writes `<name>.html`.
Read [references/review.md](references/review.md).

- If `lavish-axi` is on the PATH, run `lavish-axi <name>.html`.
  The owner approves, rejects or comments on each row and each requirement, and queues the review.
- Else give the owner the file path.
  The page puts the decisions on the clipboard ("Copy the review").
- Report the file path and the board first.
  Then wait for the feedback (`lavish-axi poll <name>.html`).
- Apply the feedback: delete rejected rows and their requirements, answer each comment, run `check` and `render` again.
  Account for each submitted id.

## Refactor

When the owner approves a proposal, do not refactor in the same step.

1. Write the plan: the rows that change, the code that changes (`file:line`), and the order.
2. Write the failing tests that pin the approved behavior of the proposal.
   Run them and see them fail.
3. Stop.
   The refactor is a separate step, which the owner approves.

## Done

- The machine file, the `check` output, the review page, and each failing test with the path that it targets.
- A short report: the proven bugs, the deleted rows and requirements, the proposal (shown or hidden, and why), and the open questions for the owner.
==> skills/state-machine/test/faults.sm.md <==
# sm-axi self-test: one known fault for each finding

## Requirements

- R1: A job can start and stop.
  — owner, test fixture
- R2: A job ends when its work is done.
  — owner, test fixture
- R3: A started job loads, then runs.
  — owner, test fixture
- R4: Any job can be cancelled.
  — owner, test fixture
- R9: Nobody uses this.
  — owner, test fixture

## Machine bugs

initial: a final: done

| state | event | guard | next | req | source |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a | go | | b | R1 | t:1 |
| a | stop | | done | R1 | t:2 |
| b | go | ok | c | R1 | t:3 |
| b | stop | | done | R1 | t:4 |
| c | go | | done | R2 | t:5 |
| c | go | | a | R2 | t:6 |
| d | go | | done | R1 | t:7 |
| d | stop | | done | | t:8 |
| a | jump | | e | R1 | t:9 |
| * | jump | | - | R1 | t:10 |

## Machine simp

initial: idle final: done

| state | event | guard | next | req | source |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| idle | start | | load | R3 | t:11 |
| load | loaded | | run | R3 | t:12 |
